/* Tekrar havuzu: rd-main, rd-detail, rd-inference, rd-dialogue */
PRACTICE.push(
  /* ---------- rd-main ---------- */
  {id:"r-rd-main-01", sub:"rd-main", from:["reading-01","w6-01"], passage:"Microplastics, fragments of plastic smaller than five millimetres, have been detected almost everywhere scientists have looked, from Arctic snow to human blood. Their presence in bottled and tap water has understandably alarmed the public. However, researchers stress that detection is not the same as harm. Most studies so far have exposed cells or animals to concentrations far higher than those people normally encounter, so their relevance to human health remains unclear. A further difficulty is that laboratories use different methods to count particles, which makes results hard to compare. The World Health Organization has therefore called not for panic but for standardised measurement techniques and long-term studies, while recommending that plastic pollution be reduced regardless of the outcome.", q:"The passage is mainly concerned with ----.", o:["the widespread presence of microplastics and the uncertainty that still surrounds their effects on human health","the proven damage that microplastics cause to human blood cells","the reasons why bottled water is safer to drink than tap water","the history of plastic production since the early twentieth century","the methods laboratories use to remove microplastics from drinking water"], a:0, e:"Metin mikroplastiklerin her yerde bulunduğunu, ancak “detection is not the same as harm” diyerek sağlık etkisinin belirsiz olduğunu anlatır; A bütünü kapsar. B metnin reddettiği kesin zararı ileri sürer; E ise “count particles” detayını “remove” olarak çarpıtır."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-02", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-05","reading-01"], passage:"Johannes Gutenberg's printing press, developed around 1450, is often credited with transforming European society almost overnight. The reality was more gradual. For several decades, printed books remained expensive, and most were Latin religious texts aimed at a small educated elite. The press began to have wider effects only when printers turned to cheaper pamphlets written in local languages. During the Reformation, Martin Luther's short tracts were reprinted so quickly that his ideas reached readers across Germany within weeks, something no handwritten copy could have achieved. Historians therefore argue that the technology alone did not change society; its impact depended on how it was used and on readers' demand for new kinds of texts.", q:"The main idea of the passage is that ----.", o:["Gutenberg's invention immediately made books affordable for ordinary people","Martin Luther was the first author to write religious texts in a local language","handwritten copies remained more popular than printed books throughout the Reformation","the Reformation would have followed exactly the same course without the printing press","the social impact of the printing press developed gradually and depended on how the technology came to be used"], a:4, e:"Metin matbaanın toplumu “bir gecede” değiştirdiği görüşünü düzeltir: etki yavaş gelmiş ve teknolojinin nasıl kullanıldığına bağlı olmuştur; E bunu verir. A metnin ilk cümlede aktarıp “The reality was more gradual” ile çürüttüğü görüştür."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-03", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-09","reading-01"], passage:"For decades, most economists taught that raising the minimum wage would inevitably cost jobs: if labour becomes more expensive, employers will hire fewer workers. In the early 1990s, two economists challenged this view by comparing fast-food restaurants in two neighbouring American states, only one of which had raised its minimum wage. Contrary to expectations, employment in the state with the higher wage did not fall. Their study provoked fierce debate, and later research has produced mixed results. A growing consensus now holds that moderate increases have little effect on employment, while very large increases, particularly in poorer regions, may indeed reduce hiring. The question, in other words, is less whether a minimum wage harms jobs than at what level it begins to do so.", q:"The passage mainly discusses ----.", o:["why fast-food restaurants pay their workers such low wages","how thinking about the employment effects of the minimum wage has moved from a simple rule to a more qualified view","the proof that raising the minimum wage never affects employment","the differences between the economies of two neighbouring American states","the reasons why poorer regions suffer from higher unemployment"], a:1, e:"Metin “her artış iş kaybettirir” görüşünden “hangi düzeyde zarar verir” sorusuna geçişi anlatır; B bu değişimi kapsar. C aşırı genellemedir; metne göre çok büyük artışlar istihdamı azaltabilir."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-04", sub:"rd-main", from:["reading-04","reading-01"], passage:"The trillions of bacteria living in the human gut were long thought to matter mainly for digestion. Over the past two decades, however, researchers have found that these microbes also communicate with the brain, producing chemicals that can influence mood and stress. In one striking experiment, mice raised without any gut bacteria reacted more strongly to stress than normal mice, and their behaviour changed when they received bacteria from other animals. Such findings have led to excitement about so-called psychobiotics, probiotic supplements that might one day treat anxiety or depression. Most researchers urge caution, though. Results in mice do not always apply to humans, and the few human trials conducted so far have been small and inconsistent.", q:"The author's main purpose in the passage is to ----.", o:["introduce evidence that gut bacteria may affect the brain while noting that its relevance to humans is still uncertain","encourage readers to take probiotic supplements to treat depression","describe how gut bacteria help the body to digest food","explain why experiments on mice are always unreliable","compare the digestive systems of mice and humans"], a:0, e:"Metin bağırsak bakterilerinin beyinle iletişimine dair bulguları sunar, ardından “Most researchers urge caution” ile insanlara uygulanabilirliğin belirsiz olduğunu belirtir; A ikisini de kapsar. B yazarın temkinli tutumunu yok sayar; C metnin terk edilen eski görüşüdür."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-05", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-01","reading-01"], passage:"Artificial light at night is one of the fastest-growing forms of pollution, yet it receives far less attention than air or water pollution. Its effects on wildlife can be severe. Many nocturnal insects are drawn to street lamps, where they circle until they die of exhaustion or are eaten, and some scientists believe this contributes to the widely reported decline in insect numbers. Migrating birds, which use the stars to navigate, can become disoriented by brightly lit buildings and collide with them. Unlike many environmental problems, however, light pollution is relatively easy to reduce. Shielding lamps so that they point downwards, switching to warmer colours and dimming lights late at night can cut its impact considerably at little cost.", q:"The passage is mainly concerned with ----.", o:["the reasons why insect populations are declining around the world","the methods migrating birds use to find their way at night","why light pollution is more dangerous than air or water pollution","the harm that artificial light causes to wildlife and the fairly simple ways in which it can be limited","the high cost of redesigning street lighting in cities"], a:3, e:"Metin ışık kirliliğinin yaban hayatına zararını ve “relatively easy to reduce” diyerek ucuz çözümlerini anlatır; D ikisini de kapsar. A tek bir detayı genelleştirir; E ise “at little cost” ifadesiyle çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-06", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-05","reading-04"], passage:"In many countries, a large proportion of all antibiotics sold is given not to people but to farm animals. For decades, farmers added small doses to animal feed, not to treat illness but because the drugs made animals grow faster. Scientists have long warned that this practice encourages the spread of resistant bacteria, which can pass to humans through food, water or direct contact. In 2006, the European Union banned the use of antibiotics for growth promotion. Denmark, which had introduced similar restrictions even earlier, offers an instructive example: its pig industry continued to expand after the ban, and levels of resistance in farm animals fell. Critics had predicted serious economic losses, but these largely failed to materialise.", q:"The main idea of the passage is that ----.", o:["antibiotics given to farm animals are the only source of resistant bacteria in humans","restricting the routine use of antibiotics in livestock can reduce resistance without the heavy economic costs that were feared","Denmark has the largest pig industry in the European Union","farmers should stop raising pigs in order to protect human health","antibiotics are no longer sold in European countries"], a:1, e:"Metin büyüme amaçlı antibiyotik kullanımının riskini ve Danimarka örneğiyle yasağın direnci azaltıp beklenen ekonomik kaybı getirmediğini anlatır; B bunu verir. A aşırı genellemedir; metin dirençli bakterilerin hayvanlardan da bulaşabildiğini söyler, tek kaynak olduğunu değil."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-07", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-09","reading-04"], passage:"When the pandemic forced millions of office workers to work from home, many commentators predicted the end of the big city. If people no longer needed to commute, why would they pay high rents in crowded urban centres? Some did leave: in several countries, house prices in small towns and rural areas rose sharply as city dwellers moved out. Yet the exodus proved smaller than expected. Most people who left moved only to suburbs within reach of the city, and many companies have since required staff to return to the office for at least part of the week. Cities, it seems, offer more than office space; restaurants, cultural life and the chance to meet new people continue to attract residents.", q:"The passage mainly discusses ----.", o:["why predictions that remote work would empty large cities have proved largely mistaken","the sharp rise in house prices in rural areas during the pandemic","the advantages of commuting to work by public transport","how companies organised remote work during the pandemic","why most city dwellers moved permanently to the countryside"], a:0, e:"Metin büyük şehirlerin sonunun geleceği tahminini aktarır ve “Yet the exodus proved smaller than expected” ile bunun büyük ölçüde yanlış çıktığını anlatır; A bütünü kapsar. B tek bir detaydır; E ise metinle çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-08", sub:"rd-main", from:["reading-04","w6-01"], passage:"For much of the twentieth century, bilingualism was considered a disadvantage for children, who were thought to become confused by learning two languages at once. From the 1960s onwards, this view was reversed, and numerous studies reported that bilingual people outperformed monolinguals on tasks requiring attention and self-control. More recently, however, the so-called bilingual advantage has itself come under scrutiny. Large studies with thousands of participants have often failed to replicate it, and some researchers suspect that journals were more willing to publish positive results than negative ones. None of this means that learning a second language is not worthwhile; its social, cultural and economic benefits are not in doubt. What is disputed is only whether it also sharpens general thinking skills.", q:"The author's main purpose in the passage is to ----.", o:["discourage parents from bringing up their children with two languages","show how the claim that bilingualism improves thinking skills has come to be questioned","prove that bilingual people have weaker attention than monolinguals","describe the economic benefits of learning a foreign language","criticise journals for refusing to publish research on language learning"], a:1, e:"Metin iki dilliliğe bakışın tarihsel değişimini anlatıp asıl olarak “bilingual advantage” iddiasının sorgulanmasına odaklanır; B bunu verir. A metnin ilk cümlesindeki eski görüştür ve yazar ikinci dil öğrenmenin değerli olduğunu açıkça söyler."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-09", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-01","reading-01"], passage:"Since the mid-2000s, beekeepers in North America and Europe have reported alarming losses of honeybee colonies, sometimes more than a third in a single winter. Early media coverage often blamed a single culprit, whether mobile phone signals or a particular group of pesticides. Scientists now agree that the picture is more complex. Parasitic mites, viral diseases, poor nutrition caused by the loss of wildflowers and exposure to pesticides all weaken colonies, and these stresses appear to reinforce one another. A colony that is already undernourished, for example, is less able to resist infection. For this reason, researchers argue that no single ban or treatment will solve the problem; only measures that address several factors at once are likely to succeed.", q:"The passage is mainly concerned with ----.", o:["the harmful effect of mobile phone signals on honeybees","the combination of interacting factors behind honeybee losses and what it means for possible solutions","the history of beekeeping in North America and Europe","why a particular group of pesticides should be banned immediately","how honeybees choose between different kinds of wildflowers"], a:1, e:"Metin arı kayıplarının birbirini güçlendiren çok sayıda nedeni olduğunu ve bu yüzden tek bir önlemin yetmeyeceğini anlatır; B bunu kapsar. A ve D metnin eleştirdiği “tek suçlu” yaklaşımını yansıtır."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-10", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-05","reading-01"], passage:"Many Roman harbour walls and buildings have survived for two thousand years, while modern concrete structures often begin to crumble within decades. Engineers long assumed that the Romans' secret lay in the volcanic ash they mixed into their concrete. Recent research suggests that this is only part of the answer. Analysis of samples has revealed small white lumps of lime that were once dismissed as a sign of careless mixing. In fact, these lumps appear to allow the material to repair itself: when water enters a crack, it dissolves some of the lime, which then hardens and seals the gap. Researchers hope that similar self-healing mixtures could extend the lifespan of modern buildings and reduce the large carbon emissions associated with cement production.", q:"The main idea of the passage is that ----.", o:["volcanic ash is the only reason why Roman buildings have lasted so long","Roman builders were often careless when mixing their concrete","new research has revealed a self-repairing mechanism that helps to explain the durability of Roman concrete","modern concrete is much stronger than the concrete used by the Romans","cement production is the main source of carbon emissions in the world"], a:2, e:"Metin Roma betonunun dayanıklılığını açıklayan yeni bulguyu, yani kendi kendini onaran kireç topaklarını anlatır; C bunu verir. A metnin “only part of the answer” diyerek düzelttiği eski varsayımdır; B ise kireç topaklarının bir zamanlar yanlış yorumlanma biçimidir."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-11", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-09","reading-01"], passage:"When microfinance emerged in Bangladesh in the 1970s, offering small loans to poor people who had no access to banks, it was hailed as a powerful weapon against poverty. Its founder later received the Nobel Peace Prize, and lenders spread across the developing world. By the 2010s, however, a series of carefully designed studies had tempered this enthusiasm. In six countries, researchers found that access to microloans helped some borrowers expand their businesses but did not, on average, raise household incomes significantly or lift families out of poverty. This does not make microfinance useless: it gives households more flexibility in managing money and coping with emergencies. It simply appears to be a modest tool rather than the revolution its supporters once promised.", q:"The passage mainly discusses ----.", o:["the life and career of the founder of microfinance","why banks refuse to lend money to poor people","the proof that microfinance has no benefits at all","how evidence has led to a more modest assessment of what microfinance can achieve","the economic history of Bangladesh since the 1970s"], a:3, e:"Metin mikrofinansa duyulan ilk coşkuyu ve araştırmaların bunu “tempered” ettiğini, yani daha ölçülü bir değerlendirmeye varıldığını anlatır; D bunu kapsar. C aşırıdır; yazar “This does not make microfinance useless” der."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-12", sub:"rd-main", from:["reading-04","w6-05"], passage:"Cultivated meat is produced by growing animal cells in large steel tanks rather than by raising and slaughtering animals. Its supporters claim it could dramatically reduce the land, water and greenhouse gas emissions associated with livestock farming. In 2020, Singapore became the first country to allow its sale. However, significant obstacles remain. The nutrient solution in which the cells grow is expensive, and producing meat at scale requires vast amounts of energy. One recent analysis even suggested that, if that energy comes from fossil fuels, cultivated meat could have a larger carbon footprint than some conventional beef. Whether the technology fulfils its environmental promise may therefore depend less on laboratory breakthroughs than on the supply of clean electricity.", q:"The author's main purpose in the passage is to ----.", o:["persuade readers to stop eating conventional beef","explain why Singapore decided to ban the sale of cultivated meat","argue that cultivated meat will never be sold commercially","describe cultivated meat and explain why its environmental benefits are not yet assured","describe the design of the steel tanks used to grow animal cells"], a:3, e:"Metin yetiştirilmiş eti tanıtır, ardından “However, significant obstacles remain” ile çevresel vaadinin enerji kaynağına bağlı olduğunu anlatır; D bunu verir. B metinle çelişir; Singapur satışa ilk izin veren ülkedir."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-13", sub:"rd-main", from:["reading-01","w6-01"], passage:"The Black Death, which killed perhaps a third of Europe's population between 1347 and 1351, was a catastrophe of almost unimaginable scale. Paradoxically, it also improved the lives of many survivors. With so many workers dead, labour became scarce, and peasants in England and elsewhere were able to demand higher wages or leave their lords in search of better conditions. Landowners and governments tried to resist: England passed laws fixing wages at pre-plague levels. These laws proved largely ineffective, however, and real wages for labourers rose substantially over the following century. Some historians argue that this shift weakened the feudal system and, in the long term, helped to prepare the ground for the economic changes of early modern Europe.", q:"The passage is mainly concerned with ----.", o:["the medical causes of the Black Death and the ways it spread","the success of English laws in controlling labourers' wages","the fall in the population of early modern Europe","the reasons why landowners supported higher wages for peasants","the unexpected economic benefits that the Black Death brought to many of those who survived it"], a:4, e:"Metnin odağı “Paradoxically, it also improved the lives of many survivors” cümlesidir: işgücü azlığı ücretleri artırmıştır; E bunu verir. B metinle çelişir, yasalar “largely ineffective” kalmıştır; D de yanlıştır, toprak sahipleri direnmiştir."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-14", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-09","reading-04"], passage:"The deep ocean floor is scattered with potato-sized nodules rich in cobalt, nickel and manganese, metals that are essential for the batteries of electric cars. Mining companies argue that collecting them would cause less damage than mining on land, where forests are cleared and local communities displaced. Marine scientists are far from convinced. The deep sea is among the least explored environments on Earth; many of the animals living on the nodules have not yet been described, and some areas disturbed in experiments decades ago still show little sign of recovery. Several governments and car manufacturers have therefore called for a pause in deep-sea mining until its environmental effects are better understood.", q:"The passage mainly discusses ----.", o:["the debate over whether the deep sea should be mined for battery metals","the design of batteries used in electric cars","the damage caused to forests and communities by mining on land","the discovery of new animal species in the deep ocean","the reasons why car manufacturers have stopped using cobalt"], a:0, e:"Metin maden şirketlerinin savını, deniz bilimcilerin itirazını ve ara verme çağrısını sunar; yani derin deniz madenciliği tartışmasını anlatır. C şirketlerin savındaki tek bir detaydır; E ise metinde yoktur."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-15", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-05","reading-01"], passage:"Many university students now take notes on laptops, which allow them to type far faster than they can write. It might seem obvious that recording more of a lecture would lead to better learning. A widely cited series of experiments suggested the opposite: students who took notes by hand performed better on conceptual questions than those who typed. The researchers argued that because writing is slow, handwriters are forced to summarise and rephrase ideas, whereas typists tend to copy lectures word for word without processing them. Later attempts to repeat the experiments, however, have produced weaker and less consistent results. The safest conclusion may be that what matters is not the tool but whether students actively think about the material as they record it.", q:"The main idea of the passage is that ----.", o:["laptops should be banned from university lectures","the way students process information while taking notes may matter more than whether they write or type","handwriting has been proven to be superior to typing in every situation","students who type take notes more slowly than those who write by hand","lectures should be recorded so that students need not take notes"], a:1, e:"Metin el yazısının üstün olduğu iddiasını aktarır, tekrar deneylerinin zayıf sonuçlarını belirtir ve aracın değil aktif düşünmenin önemli olduğu sonucuna varır; B bunu verir. C tekrar deneyleriyle çelişen bir aşırı genellemedir."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-16", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-01","reading-01"], passage:"In April 1815, Mount Tambora in present-day Indonesia erupted in the most powerful volcanic explosion in recorded history. Apart from killing tens of thousands of people locally, it threw so much ash and sulphur into the upper atmosphere that sunlight was partially blocked across the globe. The following year became known in Europe and North America as 'the year without a summer'. Snow fell in New England in June, crops failed across much of Europe, and food prices soared, leading to famine and riots. Yet people at the time had no idea that the strange weather was linked to an eruption on the other side of the world; this connection was established by scientists only in the twentieth century.", q:"The passage is mainly concerned with ----.", o:["a volcanic eruption whose effects on the climate were felt far beyond the region where it occurred","the causes of food riots in nineteenth-century Europe","the reasons why volcanoes frequently erupt in Indonesia","the history of weather forecasting in the twentieth century","the local destruction caused by a volcano in Indonesia"], a:0, e:"Metin Tambora patlamasının dünya çapındaki iklim etkilerini (Avrupa ve Kuzey Amerika'da yazsız yıl) anlatır; A bunu kapsar. E çok dardır, yalnızca yerel ölümlere değinir; B ise sonuçlardan birini ana konu gibi sunar."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-17", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-09","reading-04"], passage:"Traditional economic theory assumed that people weigh gains and losses of the same size equally. Research in behavioural economics has shown that this is not the case. In numerous experiments, people feel the pain of losing a sum of money roughly twice as strongly as the pleasure of gaining the same amount, a tendency known as loss aversion. This helps to explain behaviour that otherwise seems irrational, such as investors' reluctance to sell shares that have fallen in value, since doing so means admitting a loss. Governments have also put the idea to use. In one trial, teachers who received a bonus in advance and were told they would have to return it if their pupils' results did not improve achieved better results than teachers promised the same bonus at the end of the year.", q:"The passage mainly discusses ----.", o:["the most profitable strategies for investing in shares","how teachers' salaries are calculated in different countries","a psychological tendency that departs from traditional economic assumptions and some of its practical applications","why traditional economic theory has been completely abandoned","the effect of bonuses on pupils' motivation to learn"], a:2, e:"Metin kayıptan kaçınma eğilimini tanıtır, geleneksel varsayımla çeliştiğini belirtir ve yatırımcı ile öğretmen örnekleriyle uygulamalarını gösterir; C bunu kapsar. E öğretmen örneğini çarpıtır; ikramiye öğretmenlere verilmiştir, öğrencilere değil."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-18", sub:"rd-main", from:["reading-04","w6-05"], passage:"For much of the twentieth century, land managers in Australia and North America treated all wildfires as enemies to be put out as quickly as possible. This approach had an unintended consequence: dead wood and dry vegetation built up in forests, so that when fires did break out, they burned far more intensely. Indigenous peoples in both regions had long followed a different practice, setting small, cool fires in the right seasons to clear undergrowth and create a patchwork of burned and unburned land. After a series of devastating fire seasons, governments have begun to hire Indigenous rangers and adopt similar methods. Although controlled burning cannot prevent every disaster, especially as the climate grows hotter and drier, it can significantly reduce the fuel available to large fires.", q:"The author's main purpose in the passage is to ----.", o:["argue that all wildfires should be put out as quickly as possible","describe the effects of climate change on Australian agriculture","show that controlled burning can prevent every wildfire","explain why traditional Indigenous burning practices are being revived as a way of managing wildfires","compare the forests of Australia with those of North America"], a:3, e:"Metin söndürme politikasının istenmeyen sonucunu ve yerli halkların kontrollü yakma yönteminin neden yeniden benimsendiğini anlatır; D bunu verir. C aşırı genellemedir; metin “cannot prevent every disaster” der. A ise eleştirilen eski politikadır."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-19", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-01","w6-09"], passage:"Gene-editing techniques such as CRISPR allow scientists to make precise changes to a plant's own DNA, for example switching off a gene that makes wheat vulnerable to a fungal disease. Unlike earlier genetically modified crops, edited plants often contain no genes from other species, and the changes they carry could in principle have arisen through natural mutation or conventional breeding. For this reason, several countries, including Argentina and Japan, regulate them much like traditionally bred varieties. The European Union initially took the opposite view, ruling in 2018 that edited crops should face the same strict rules as other genetically modified organisms. Many European scientists criticised the decision, warning that it would push research abroad, and the EU has since moved to relax the rules for some edited plants.", q:"The passage is mainly concerned with ----.", o:["the causes of fungal diseases in wheat","the reasons why Japanese farmers prefer traditional breeding methods","how gene-edited crops differ from earlier genetically modified crops and how different countries have chosen to regulate them","the health risks of eating genetically modified food","the history of plant breeding in Argentina"], a:2, e:"Metin gen düzenlemeli bitkilerin eski GDO'lardan farkını ve ülkelerin bunları nasıl düzenlediğini anlatır; C bütünü kapsar. A yalnızca bir örnektir; D ise metinde hiç tartışılmaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-20", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-05","reading-01"], passage:"Loneliness is usually thought of as an emotional problem rather than a medical one. A growing body of research, however, suggests that it can have serious consequences for physical health. Large studies following people over many years have linked chronic loneliness and social isolation to higher rates of heart disease, stroke and dementia, and to a risk of early death comparable to that of well-known factors such as obesity. Researchers believe that long-term loneliness keeps the body in a state of stress, raising blood pressure and weakening the immune system. In response, the United Kingdom appointed a minister for loneliness in 2018, and some doctors now practise 'social prescribing', referring isolated patients to community groups instead of, or as well as, prescribing medication.", q:"The main idea of the passage is that ----.", o:["obesity is a far more serious health risk than loneliness","community groups should replace all forms of medication","loneliness is a purely emotional problem","the United Kingdom has solved the problem of social isolation","loneliness is increasingly recognised as a threat to physical health that requires a public response"], a:4, e:"Metin yalnızlığın fiziksel sağlığa zararını ortaya koyan araştırmaları ve buna verilen kamusal tepkileri (bakan, sosyal reçete) anlatır; E bunu verir. C metnin “however” ile çürüttüğü yaygın görüştür; B ise “instead of, or as well as” ifadesini aşırı genelleştirir."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-21", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-09","reading-01"], passage:"Easter Island, famous for its giant stone statues, has often been presented as a warning about environmental self-destruction. According to this popular account, the islanders cut down all their trees to move the statues, causing soil erosion, famine and a violent collapse of their society. Recent research has challenged much of this story. Radiocarbon dating suggests that the island was settled later than once thought, and rats introduced by the first settlers may have destroyed the forest by eating palm seeds. Moreover, studies of ancient farming sites indicate that the population remained relatively stable until Europeans arrived in 1722, bringing diseases and, later, slave raids that devastated the community. Some researchers now see the islanders as an example of resilience rather than recklessness.", q:"The passage mainly discusses ----.", o:["new evidence that questions the traditional story of Easter Island's self-inflicted collapse","the techniques the islanders used to carve and move their statues","how rats spread across the islands of the Pacific","why European explorers first visited Easter Island in 1722","the dangers of deforestation for modern societies"], a:0, e:"Metin Paskalya Adası'nın “kendi kendini yok etme” hikâyesini aktarır ve “Recent research has challenged much of this story” ile yeni kanıtları sunar; A bunu kapsar. C yalnızca bir detaydır; E ise metnin sorguladığı “uyarı” yorumunu genelleştirir."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-22", sub:"rd-main", from:["reading-04","w6-05"], passage:"In recent years, popular books and documentaries have described forests as cooperative communities in which trees 'talk' to one another through underground fungal networks, sharing sugar with their offspring and warning neighbours of insect attacks. The idea is appealing, and it rests on real discoveries: fungi do connect the roots of many trees, and nutrients can move between them. However, a recent review of the evidence concluded that many of the most striking claims go well beyond what field studies have shown. Several influential experiments involved small numbers of seedlings, and the amounts of carbon transferred were often too small to be of clear benefit. The reviewers did not deny that fungal networks exist; they argued that their role has been exaggerated in public discussion.", q:"The author's main purpose in the passage is to ----.", o:["prove that fungal networks between trees do not exist","explain how trees protect themselves against insect attacks","show that popular claims about communication between trees go further than the scientific evidence supports","praise documentaries for making science accessible to the public","describe how seedlings absorb carbon from the soil"], a:2, e:"Metin ağaçların “konuştuğu” popüler görüşünü aktarır ve bir incelemenin bu iddiaların kanıtları aştığını gösterdiğini anlatır; C bunu verir. A aşırıdır; metin “did not deny that fungal networks exist” der."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-23", sub:"rd-main", from:["reading-01","w6-09"], passage:"Universal basic income, a regular cash payment given to every citizen regardless of whether they work, has attracted supporters across the political spectrum. Critics have long objected that such payments would discourage people from working. Evidence from trials offers some reassurance on this point. In Finland, two thousand unemployed people received a monthly payment without conditions between 2017 and 2018; they reported greater well-being and were, if anything, slightly more likely to find jobs than a comparison group. Nevertheless, the trials have been small and temporary, and participants knew the payments would end. Whether people would behave in the same way if they received the money for life, and how governments could afford it for everyone, remain open questions.", q:"The passage is mainly concerned with ----.", o:["the causes of unemployment in Finland","why universal basic income is supported only by one political group","the proof that a universal basic income is affordable","the reasons why people stop working when they receive cash","what trials have and have not revealed about the effects of a universal basic income"], a:4, e:"Metin deneylerin iş isteğiyle ilgili kaygıyı hafiflettiğini ama “Nevertheless” ile sınırlı olduğunu ve açık soruların kaldığını anlatır; E bu iki yönü kapsar. B “across the political spectrum” ile çelişir; D ise eleştirmenlerin deneylerle desteklenmeyen iddiasıdır."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-24", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-05","reading-04"], passage:"Fears that machines will destroy jobs are almost as old as industrialisation itself. In the early nineteenth century, English textile workers known as Luddites smashed the new mechanical looms they blamed for their falling wages. In the long run, their fears proved mistaken for the economy as a whole: mechanisation lowered the price of cloth, demand soared, and far more people eventually worked in the textile industry than before. Economists often cite this history to argue that technology creates at least as many jobs as it destroys. Yet the argument has limits. The new jobs did not necessarily go to the workers who had lost their old ones, and for many families the transition brought decades of hardship. Today's debates about artificial intelligence may need to take both lessons into account.", q:"The main idea of the passage is that ----.", o:["the Luddites were right that machines would reduce employment in the textile industry","artificial intelligence will destroy more jobs than it creates","the price of cloth fell because workers destroyed the new looms","economists have always opposed the introduction of new technologies","although technology has tended to create jobs overall, the transition can be painful for the workers directly affected"], a:4, e:"Metin teknolojinin genelde iş yarattığını kabul eder, ama “Yet the argument has limits” ile geçişin işçiler için acı verici olabileceğini ekler; E iki yönü kapsar. A yanlıştır; metne göre Luddite'ların korkusu ekonominin geneli için “mistaken” çıkmıştır."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-25", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-09","w6-01"], passage:"Tardigrades, microscopic animals less than a millimetre long, are famous for their extraordinary toughness. When their environment dries out, they curl up, lose almost all the water in their bodies and enter a state in which their metabolism nearly stops. In this condition, they have survived temperatures close to absolute zero, intense radiation and even exposure to the vacuum of space. Researchers have identified a special protein that appears to protect their DNA from damage. This discovery has attracted interest beyond biology: scientists are exploring whether similar molecules could help to preserve vaccines without refrigeration or protect human cells during radiation therapy. Such applications remain experimental, but they show how the study of obscure organisms can lead to practical benefits.", q:"The passage mainly discusses ----.", o:["the dangers of radiation therapy for cancer patients","the reasons why tardigrades were sent into space","the remarkable survival abilities of tardigrades and their possible practical value","the methods currently used to keep vaccines cold","why most microscopic animals cannot survive a drought"], a:2, e:"Metin tardigradların olağanüstü dayanıklılığını ve DNA'yı koruyan proteinin olası uygulamalarını anlatır; C ikisini de kapsar. A ve D olası uygulamalardaki tek bir detayı ana konu gibi sunar."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-26", sub:"rd-main", from:["reading-04","w6-01"], passage:"Of the roughly seven thousand languages spoken today, linguists estimate that around half may disappear by the end of this century. Most are spoken by small communities whose children increasingly grow up using a national or global language instead. The loss matters for more than sentimental reasons. Each language encodes knowledge accumulated over generations, including detailed names for local plants, animals and medicines that may exist nowhere else. Revival is difficult but not impossible. Hebrew, which for centuries was used mainly in religious and scholarly texts, became the everyday language of millions in the twentieth century, and communities from Wales to New Zealand have increased the number of young speakers through schools that teach entirely in the endangered language.", q:"The author's main purpose in the passage is to ----.", o:["argue that global languages should not be taught in schools","draw attention to the threat facing many languages, explain why it matters and show that decline can be reversed","describe the history of the Hebrew language in detail","compare the education systems of Wales and New Zealand","prove that all endangered languages can easily be saved"], a:1, e:"Metin dillerin yok olma tehlikesini, bunun neden önemli olduğunu ve canlandırmanın mümkün olduğunu anlatır; B üç aşamayı kapsar. E aşırı genellemedir; metin canlandırmanın “difficult” olduğunu söyler. C ise tek bir örneği ana konu yapar."},
  {id:"r-rd-main-27", sub:"rd-main", from:["w6-01","w6-09"], passage:"Until the middle of the nineteenth century, most towns set their clocks by the position of the sun, so that noon in one town might be several minutes earlier or later than noon in a town to the east or west. This caused few problems when travel was slow. The arrival of the railways changed that. Timetables became confusing when every station kept its own time, and mistakes could lead to accidents. In Britain, railway companies adopted a single standard time based on Greenwich in the 1840s, and most towns soon followed, although the new time was not made legally binding until 1880. In 1884, an international conference established Greenwich as the starting point for a worldwide system of time zones.", q:"The passage is mainly concerned with ----.", o:["the causes of railway accidents in nineteenth-century Britain","the methods used to measure the position of the sun","why most towns refused to accept Greenwich time","how the growth of the railways led to the standardisation of time","the history of clock-making in the nineteenth century"], a:3, e:"Metin yerel güneş saatinden demiryolları yüzünden standart saate ve saat dilimlerine geçişi anlatır; D bunu kapsar. A tek bir detaydır; C ise “most towns soon followed” ile çelişir."},

  /* ---------- rd-detail ---------- */
  {id:"r-rd-detail-01", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-02","reading-02"], passage:"Microplastics, fragments of plastic smaller than five millimetres, have been detected almost everywhere scientists have looked, from Arctic snow to human blood. Their presence in bottled and tap water has understandably alarmed the public. However, researchers stress that detection is not the same as harm. Most studies so far have exposed cells or animals to concentrations far higher than those people normally encounter, so their relevance to human health remains unclear. A further difficulty is that laboratories use different methods to count particles, which makes results hard to compare. The World Health Organization has therefore called not for panic but for standardised measurement techniques and long-term studies, while recommending that plastic pollution be reduced regardless of the outcome.", q:"According to the passage, the results of studies on microplastics are hard to compare because ----.", o:["most of the studies have been carried out on samples of human blood","laboratories do not all rely on the same techniques to count the particles","the World Health Organization has refused to fund long-term research","the concentration of particles in tap water changes from day to day","microplastics have so far been found only in Arctic regions"], a:1, e:"“laboratories use different methods to count particles, which makes results hard to compare” cümlesinin yeniden ifadesi B şıkkıdır. C yanlıştır; DSÖ araştırmayı reddetmemiş, tersine uzun vadeli çalışma çağrısı yapmıştır."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-02", sub:"rd-detail", from:["reading-02","w6-06"], passage:"Johannes Gutenberg's printing press, developed around 1450, is often credited with transforming European society almost overnight. The reality was more gradual. For several decades, printed books remained expensive, and most were Latin religious texts aimed at a small educated elite. The press began to have wider effects only when printers turned to cheaper pamphlets written in local languages. During the Reformation, Martin Luther's short tracts were reprinted so quickly that his ideas reached readers across Germany within weeks, something no handwritten copy could have achieved. Historians therefore argue that the technology alone did not change society; its impact depended on how it was used and on readers' demand for new kinds of texts.", q:"According to the passage, during the first decades after its invention, the printing press ----.", o:["was used chiefly to print cheap pamphlets in local languages","spread Luther's ideas across Germany within a few weeks","was banned by religious authorities in most of Europe","mainly produced costly Latin religious books for a limited readership","made handwritten books unnecessary almost at once"], a:3, e:"“For several decades, printed books remained expensive, and most were Latin religious texts aimed at a small educated elite” cümlesi D şıkkında eş anlamlılarla verilmiştir. A ve B matbaanın daha sonraki dönemine aittir; soru ilk on yılları soruyor."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-03", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-06","reading-02"], passage:"For decades, most economists taught that raising the minimum wage would inevitably cost jobs: if labour becomes more expensive, employers will hire fewer workers. In the early 1990s, two economists challenged this view by comparing fast-food restaurants in two neighbouring American states, only one of which had raised its minimum wage. Contrary to expectations, employment in the state with the higher wage did not fall. Their study provoked fierce debate, and later research has produced mixed results. A growing consensus now holds that moderate increases have little effect on employment, while very large increases, particularly in poorer regions, may indeed reduce hiring. The question, in other words, is less whether a minimum wage harms jobs than at what level it begins to do so.", q:"According to the passage, the study carried out in the early 1990s found that ----.", o:["fast-food restaurants moved to the state where wages were lower","very large wage increases reduced hiring in poorer regions","jobs were not lost in the state that had increased its minimum wage","employment fell at the same rate in both states","most economists immediately accepted its conclusions"], a:2, e:"“employment in the state with the higher wage did not fall” ifadesi C şıkkında yeniden yazılmıştır. B bugünkü uzlaşıya ait bir bilgidir, 1990'lardaki çalışmanın bulgusu değildir; E ise “fierce debate” ile çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-04", sub:"rd-detail", from:["reading-02","w6-10"], passage:"The trillions of bacteria living in the human gut were long thought to matter mainly for digestion. Over the past two decades, however, researchers have found that these microbes also communicate with the brain, producing chemicals that can influence mood and stress. In one striking experiment, mice raised without any gut bacteria reacted more strongly to stress than normal mice, and their behaviour changed when they received bacteria from other animals. Such findings have led to excitement about so-called psychobiotics, probiotic supplements that might one day treat anxiety or depression. Most researchers urge caution, though. Results in mice do not always apply to humans, and the few human trials conducted so far have been small and inconsistent.", q:"According to the passage, mice raised without gut bacteria ----.", o:["were unable to digest their food properly","were later used in the first human trials of psychobiotics","produced chemicals that lowered their stress levels","responded more strongly to stress than ordinary mice","behaved in exactly the same way as normal mice"], a:3, e:"“mice raised without any gut bacteria reacted more strongly to stress than normal mice” cümlesi D şıkkında eş anlamlılarla verilmiştir. A bakterilerin sindirimdeki rolünden çıkarılan ama metinde yer almayan bir detaydır."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-05", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-04"], passage:"Artificial light at night is one of the fastest-growing forms of pollution, yet it receives far less attention than air or water pollution. Its effects on wildlife can be severe. Many nocturnal insects are drawn to street lamps, where they circle until they die of exhaustion or are eaten, and some scientists believe this contributes to the widely reported decline in insect numbers. Migrating birds, which use the stars to navigate, can become disoriented by brightly lit buildings and collide with them. Unlike many environmental problems, however, light pollution is relatively easy to reduce. Shielding lamps so that they point downwards, switching to warmer colours and dimming lights late at night can cut its impact considerably at little cost.", q:"The word “disoriented” in the passage is closest in meaning to ----.", o:["seriously injured","strongly attracted","extremely tired","frightened","confused about direction"], a:4, e:"Yıldızlarla yön bulan kuşların parlak binalar yüzünden “disoriented” olup çarpması, yön duygusunu kaybetmeleri anlamına gelir. “strongly attracted” böceklerin lambalara çekilmesinden dolayı çekicidir ama o cümle “disoriented” kelimesini açıklamaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-06", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-02","reading-05"], passage:"In many countries, a large proportion of all antibiotics sold is given not to people but to farm animals. For decades, farmers added small doses to animal feed, not to treat illness but because the drugs made animals grow faster. Scientists have long warned that this practice encourages the spread of resistant bacteria, which can pass to humans through food, water or direct contact. In 2006, the European Union banned the use of antibiotics for growth promotion. Denmark, which had introduced similar restrictions even earlier, offers an instructive example: its pig industry continued to expand after the ban, and levels of resistance in farm animals fell. Critics had predicted serious economic losses, but these largely failed to materialise.", q:"According to the passage, farmers traditionally added antibiotics to animal feed in order to ----.", o:["treat infections that had spread from humans","prevent bacteria from entering water supplies","comply with the requirements of European law","make their animals gain weight more quickly","improve the taste of the meat they sold"], a:3, e:"“not to treat illness but because the drugs made animals grow faster” ifadesi D şıkkıyla karşılanır. A tam da metnin “not to treat illness” diyerek dışladığı amaçtır."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-07", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-06","w6-02"], passage:"When the pandemic forced millions of office workers to work from home, many commentators predicted the end of the big city. If people no longer needed to commute, why would they pay high rents in crowded urban centres? Some did leave: in several countries, house prices in small towns and rural areas rose sharply as city dwellers moved out. Yet the exodus proved smaller than expected. Most people who left moved only to suburbs within reach of the city, and many companies have since required staff to return to the office for at least part of the week. Cities, it seems, offer more than office space; restaurants, cultural life and the chance to meet new people continue to attract residents.", q:"According to the passage, most people who moved out of cities ----.", o:["bought houses in remote rural villages","returned to the city centre within a few months","were forced to leave by their employers","settled in suburbs that were still close enough to the city","no longer needed to work at all"], a:3, e:"“Most people who left moved only to suburbs within reach of the city” cümlesi D şıkkında yeniden ifade edilmiştir. A kırsalda fiyatların arttığı bilgisinden çekici hâle gelir ama metin taşınanların çoğunun banliyöye gittiğini söyler."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-08", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-02","reading-02"], passage:"For much of the twentieth century, bilingualism was considered a disadvantage for children, who were thought to become confused by learning two languages at once. From the 1960s onwards, this view was reversed, and numerous studies reported that bilingual people outperformed monolinguals on tasks requiring attention and self-control. More recently, however, the so-called bilingual advantage has itself come under scrutiny. Large studies with thousands of participants have often failed to replicate it, and some researchers suspect that journals were more willing to publish positive results than negative ones. None of this means that learning a second language is not worthwhile; its social, cultural and economic benefits are not in doubt. What is disputed is only whether it also sharpens general thinking skills.", q:"According to the passage, one possible reason why the bilingual advantage was widely reported is that ----.", o:["children who learnt two languages at once became confused","large studies with thousands of participants confirmed it","monolingual people refused to take part in the experiments","learning a second language brings obvious economic benefits","studies showing positive effects were more likely to be published"], a:4, e:"“journals were more willing to publish positive results than negative ones” ifadesi E şıkkında yeniden yazılmıştır. B tam tersidir; büyük çalışmalar etkiyi çoğu zaman tekrarlayamamıştır (failed to replicate)."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-09", sub:"rd-detail", from:["reading-02","w6-06"], passage:"Since the mid-2000s, beekeepers in North America and Europe have reported alarming losses of honeybee colonies, sometimes more than a third in a single winter. Early media coverage often blamed a single culprit, whether mobile phone signals or a particular group of pesticides. Scientists now agree that the picture is more complex. Parasitic mites, viral diseases, poor nutrition caused by the loss of wildflowers and exposure to pesticides all weaken colonies, and these stresses appear to reinforce one another. A colony that is already undernourished, for example, is less able to resist infection. For this reason, researchers argue that no single ban or treatment will solve the problem; only measures that address several factors at once are likely to succeed.", q:"According to the passage, a colony suffering from poor nutrition ----.", o:["is less capable of fighting off disease","is protected against parasitic mites","produces more honey during the winter","is usually the result of mobile phone signals","recovers quickly once pesticides are banned"], a:0, e:"“A colony that is already undernourished … is less able to resist infection” cümlesi A şıkkında eş anlamlılarla verilmiştir. D metnin erken medya haberlerine ait, bilim insanlarınca reddedilen bir açıklamadır."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-10", sub:"rd-detail", from:["reading-02","w6-10"], passage:"Many Roman harbour walls and buildings have survived for two thousand years, while modern concrete structures often begin to crumble within decades. Engineers long assumed that the Romans' secret lay in the volcanic ash they mixed into their concrete. Recent research suggests that this is only part of the answer. Analysis of samples has revealed small white lumps of lime that were once dismissed as a sign of careless mixing. In fact, these lumps appear to allow the material to repair itself: when water enters a crack, it dissolves some of the lime, which then hardens and seals the gap. Researchers hope that similar self-healing mixtures could extend the lifespan of modern buildings and reduce the large carbon emissions associated with cement production.", q:"According to the passage, the small white lumps of lime found in Roman concrete ----.", o:["prove that Roman workers mixed their materials carelessly","were added in order to reduce carbon emissions","help to fill cracks when they come into contact with water","cause the concrete to dissolve gradually in water","were first discovered in modern harbour walls"], a:2, e:"Su çatlağa girince kirecin bir kısmını çözer, kireç sertleşir ve boşluğu kapatır; C bunu eş anlamlılarla verir. A topakların eskiden “dismissed as a sign of careless mixing” şeklinde yanlış yorumlanmasıdır; metin bu görüşü “In fact” ile çürütür."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-11", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-10","reading-05"], passage:"When microfinance emerged in Bangladesh in the 1970s, offering small loans to poor people who had no access to banks, it was hailed as a powerful weapon against poverty. Its founder later received the Nobel Peace Prize, and lenders spread across the developing world. By the 2010s, however, a series of carefully designed studies had tempered this enthusiasm. In six countries, researchers found that access to microloans helped some borrowers expand their businesses but did not, on average, raise household incomes significantly or lift families out of poverty. This does not make microfinance useless: it gives households more flexibility in managing money and coping with emergencies. It simply appears to be a modest tool rather than the revolution its supporters once promised.", q:"According to the passage, the studies carried out in six countries showed that microloans ----.", o:["allowed some borrowers to grow their businesses without significantly increasing average household income","lifted most of the families who received them out of poverty","were not available anywhere in the developing world","made it harder for households to deal with emergencies","were more successful in Bangladesh than in any other country"], a:0, e:"“helped some borrowers expand their businesses but did not, on average, raise household incomes significantly” cümlesi A şıkkında yeniden yazılmıştır. B destekçilerin ilk beklentisidir; araştırmalar bunun gerçekleşmediğini gösterir."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-12", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-02","w6-06"], passage:"Cultivated meat is produced by growing animal cells in large steel tanks rather than by raising and slaughtering animals. Its supporters claim it could dramatically reduce the land, water and greenhouse gas emissions associated with livestock farming. In 2020, Singapore became the first country to allow its sale. However, significant obstacles remain. The nutrient solution in which the cells grow is expensive, and producing meat at scale requires vast amounts of energy. One recent analysis even suggested that, if that energy comes from fossil fuels, cultivated meat could have a larger carbon footprint than some conventional beef. Whether the technology fulfils its environmental promise may therefore depend less on laboratory breakthroughs than on the supply of clean electricity.", q:"According to the passage, one obstacle facing cultivated meat is that ----.", o:["it cannot be sold legally in any country","the liquid used to feed the growing cells is costly","it requires more land than conventional livestock farming","most consumers find its taste unpleasant","it cannot be produced without slaughtering animals"], a:1, e:"“The nutrient solution in which the cells grow is expensive” cümlesi B şıkkında eş anlamlılarla verilmiştir. A yanlıştır; Singapur 2020'de satışa izin vermiştir. D metinde hiç geçmez."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-13", sub:"rd-detail", from:["reading-05","w6-10"], passage:"The Black Death, which killed perhaps a third of Europe's population between 1347 and 1351, was a catastrophe of almost unimaginable scale. Paradoxically, it also improved the lives of many survivors. With so many workers dead, labour became scarce, and peasants in England and elsewhere were able to demand higher wages or leave their lords in search of better conditions. Landowners and governments tried to resist: England passed laws fixing wages at pre-plague levels. These laws proved largely ineffective, however, and real wages for labourers rose substantially over the following century. Some historians argue that this shift weakened the feudal system and, in the long term, helped to prepare the ground for the economic changes of early modern Europe.", q:"According to the passage, the laws England passed after the plague ----.", o:["largely failed to keep wages at their earlier level","raised labourers' real wages substantially","allowed peasants to leave their lords freely","were supported by most of the peasants","brought the feudal system to an immediate end"], a:0, e:"Yasalar ücretleri veba öncesi düzeyde sabitlemeyi amaçlamış ama “largely ineffective” olmuştur; A bunu verir. B çekicidir çünkü ücretler gerçekten artmıştır, ama bu yasaların sonucu değil, onlara rağmen olmuştur."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-14", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-06","reading-05"], passage:"The deep ocean floor is scattered with potato-sized nodules rich in cobalt, nickel and manganese, metals that are essential for the batteries of electric cars. Mining companies argue that collecting them would cause less damage than mining on land, where forests are cleared and local communities displaced. Marine scientists are far from convinced. The deep sea is among the least explored environments on Earth; many of the animals living on the nodules have not yet been described, and some areas disturbed in experiments decades ago still show little sign of recovery. Several governments and car manufacturers have therefore called for a pause in deep-sea mining until its environmental effects are better understood.", q:"According to the passage, areas of the sea floor disturbed in earlier experiments ----.", o:["have been fully restored by marine scientists","now contain more nodules than undisturbed areas","have recovered very little, if at all","are currently being mined by private companies","were deliberately cleared of all animal life"], a:2, e:"“some areas disturbed in experiments decades ago still show little sign of recovery” ifadesi C şıkkında yeniden yazılmıştır. A bu bilginin tam tersidir."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-15", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-02","reading-02"], passage:"Many university students now take notes on laptops, which allow them to type far faster than they can write. It might seem obvious that recording more of a lecture would lead to better learning. A widely cited series of experiments suggested the opposite: students who took notes by hand performed better on conceptual questions than those who typed. The researchers argued that because writing is slow, handwriters are forced to summarise and rephrase ideas, whereas typists tend to copy lectures word for word without processing them. Later attempts to repeat the experiments, however, have produced weaker and less consistent results. The safest conclusion may be that what matters is not the tool but whether students actively think about the material as they record it.", q:"According to the researchers mentioned in the passage, students who type their notes ----.", o:["are forced to summarise the lecturer's ideas in their own words","perform better on conceptual questions","record less of the lecture than those who use pen and paper","remember more of the lecture several months later","often reproduce what they hear without really thinking about it"], a:4, e:"“typists tend to copy lectures word for word without processing them” cümlesi E şıkkında eş anlamlılarla verilmiştir. A el ile not alanlara ait özelliktir; özneler karıştırılmıştır."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-16", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-06","reading-02"], passage:"In April 1815, Mount Tambora in present-day Indonesia erupted in the most powerful volcanic explosion in recorded history. Apart from killing tens of thousands of people locally, it threw so much ash and sulphur into the upper atmosphere that sunlight was partially blocked across the globe. The following year became known in Europe and North America as 'the year without a summer'. Snow fell in New England in June, crops failed across much of Europe, and food prices soared, leading to famine and riots. Yet people at the time had no idea that the strange weather was linked to an eruption on the other side of the world; this connection was established by scientists only in the twentieth century.", q:"According to the passage, the eruption affected the global climate because ----.", o:["it caused snow to fall across Indonesia","the material it released into the atmosphere reduced the amount of sunlight reaching the Earth","it destroyed crops across much of Asia","it raised temperatures in Europe and North America","it was followed by several smaller eruptions"], a:1, e:"“threw so much ash and sulphur into the upper atmosphere that sunlight was partially blocked” ifadesi B şıkkında yeniden yazılmıştır. D tam tersidir; sonuç yazın olmaması, yani soğumadır."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-17", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-02","reading-05"], passage:"Traditional economic theory assumed that people weigh gains and losses of the same size equally. Research in behavioural economics has shown that this is not the case. In numerous experiments, people feel the pain of losing a sum of money roughly twice as strongly as the pleasure of gaining the same amount, a tendency known as loss aversion. This helps to explain behaviour that otherwise seems irrational, such as investors' reluctance to sell shares that have fallen in value, since doing so means admitting a loss. Governments have also put the idea to use. In one trial, teachers who received a bonus in advance and were told they would have to return it if their pupils' results did not improve achieved better results than teachers promised the same bonus at the end of the year.", q:"According to the passage, investors are often unwilling to sell shares that have lost value because ----.", o:["they expect the government to compensate them","they value gains twice as much as losses","traditional economic theory advises them to keep them","selling shares involves high fees","doing so would mean accepting that they have made a loss"], a:4, e:"“since doing so means admitting a loss” ifadesi E şıkkında eş anlamlılarla verilmiştir. B metindeki oranı tersine çevirir; insanlar kaybı kazançtan iki kat güçlü hisseder."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-18", sub:"rd-detail", from:["reading-02","w6-10"], passage:"For much of the twentieth century, land managers in Australia and North America treated all wildfires as enemies to be put out as quickly as possible. This approach had an unintended consequence: dead wood and dry vegetation built up in forests, so that when fires did break out, they burned far more intensely. Indigenous peoples in both regions had long followed a different practice, setting small, cool fires in the right seasons to clear undergrowth and create a patchwork of burned and unburned land. After a series of devastating fire seasons, governments have begun to hire Indigenous rangers and adopt similar methods. Although controlled burning cannot prevent every disaster, especially as the climate grows hotter and drier, it can significantly reduce the fuel available to large fires.", q:"According to the passage, the policy of putting out all wildfires ----.", o:["was first introduced by Indigenous peoples","reduced the amount of dead wood in forests","led to a build-up of material that made later fires more intense","was abandoned before the twentieth century began","succeeded in preventing large fires for several decades"], a:2, e:"“dead wood and dry vegetation built up in forests, so that … they burned far more intensely” ifadesi C şıkkında yeniden yazılmıştır. B bu sonucun tam tersidir; A ise yerli halkların farklı bir uygulama izlediğini söyleyen metinle çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-19", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-04"], passage:"Gene-editing techniques such as CRISPR allow scientists to make precise changes to a plant's own DNA, for example switching off a gene that makes wheat vulnerable to a fungal disease. Unlike earlier genetically modified crops, edited plants often contain no genes from other species, and the changes they carry could in principle have arisen through natural mutation or conventional breeding. For this reason, several countries, including Argentina and Japan, regulate them much like traditionally bred varieties. The European Union initially took the opposite view, ruling in 2018 that edited crops should face the same strict rules as other genetically modified organisms. Many European scientists criticised the decision, warning that it would push research abroad, and the EU has since moved to relax the rules for some edited plants.", q:"The word “relax” in the passage is closest in meaning to ----.", o:["ease","tighten","clarify","abolish","review"], a:0, e:"AB'nin 2018'deki katı kararının eleştirilmesinin ardından kuralları “relax” etmesi, onları gevşetmesi (ease) anlamına gelir. D “abolish” çekicidir ama metin kuralların kaldırıldığını değil, yalnızca bazı bitkiler için yumuşatıldığını söyler."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-20", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-06","reading-02"], passage:"Loneliness is usually thought of as an emotional problem rather than a medical one. A growing body of research, however, suggests that it can have serious consequences for physical health. Large studies following people over many years have linked chronic loneliness and social isolation to higher rates of heart disease, stroke and dementia, and to a risk of early death comparable to that of well-known factors such as obesity. Researchers believe that long-term loneliness keeps the body in a state of stress, raising blood pressure and weakening the immune system. In response, the United Kingdom appointed a minister for loneliness in 2018, and some doctors now practise 'social prescribing', referring isolated patients to community groups instead of, or as well as, prescribing medication.", q:"According to the passage, researchers believe that chronic loneliness damages physical health by ----.", o:["keeping the body under prolonged stress","causing people to eat more and gain weight","reducing people's access to medication","preventing people from joining community groups","lowering blood pressure to dangerous levels"], a:0, e:"“long-term loneliness keeps the body in a state of stress” cümlesi A şıkkında eş anlamlılarla verilmiştir. B obeziteden yalnızca risk düzeyini karşılaştırmak için söz edildiğinden çekicidir; E ise “raising blood pressure” ile çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-21", sub:"rd-detail", from:["reading-02","w6-06"], passage:"Easter Island, famous for its giant stone statues, has often been presented as a warning about environmental self-destruction. According to this popular account, the islanders cut down all their trees to move the statues, causing soil erosion, famine and a violent collapse of their society. Recent research has challenged much of this story. Radiocarbon dating suggests that the island was settled later than once thought, and rats introduced by the first settlers may have destroyed the forest by eating palm seeds. Moreover, studies of ancient farming sites indicate that the population remained relatively stable until Europeans arrived in 1722, bringing diseases and, later, slave raids that devastated the community. Some researchers now see the islanders as an example of resilience rather than recklessness.", q:"According to the passage, recent research suggests that the island's forest ----.", o:["was cut down by the islanders so that they could move the statues","survived until the arrival of the Europeans","was replaced by farming sites after 1722","may have been destroyed largely by rats that arrived with the first settlers","was burned down during the slave raids"], a:3, e:"“rats introduced by the first settlers may have destroyed the forest by eating palm seeds” cümlesi D şıkkında verilmiştir. A metnin çürüttüğü popüler anlatıdır; soru ise yeni araştırmaları soruyor."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-22", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-10","reading-05"], passage:"In recent years, popular books and documentaries have described forests as cooperative communities in which trees 'talk' to one another through underground fungal networks, sharing sugar with their offspring and warning neighbours of insect attacks. The idea is appealing, and it rests on real discoveries: fungi do connect the roots of many trees, and nutrients can move between them. However, a recent review of the evidence concluded that many of the most striking claims go well beyond what field studies have shown. Several influential experiments involved small numbers of seedlings, and the amounts of carbon transferred were often too small to be of clear benefit. The reviewers did not deny that fungal networks exist; they argued that their role has been exaggerated in public discussion.", q:"According to the passage, the recent review found that ----.", o:["fungi do not in fact connect the roots of trees","trees regularly warn their neighbours about insect attacks","large-scale field studies have confirmed the popular claims","in several influential experiments, the quantity of carbon passed between trees was too small to be clearly useful","nutrients are never transferred between trees"], a:3, e:"“the amounts of carbon transferred were often too small to be of clear benefit” ifadesi D şıkkında yeniden yazılmıştır. A ve E metnin “fungi do connect the roots … nutrients can move” diyerek kabul ettiği bilgilerle çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-23", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-06","reading-05"], passage:"Universal basic income, a regular cash payment given to every citizen regardless of whether they work, has attracted supporters across the political spectrum. Critics have long objected that such payments would discourage people from working. Evidence from trials offers some reassurance on this point. In Finland, two thousand unemployed people received a monthly payment without conditions between 2017 and 2018; they reported greater well-being and were, if anything, slightly more likely to find jobs than a comparison group. Nevertheless, the trials have been small and temporary, and participants knew the payments would end. Whether people would behave in the same way if they received the money for life, and how governments could afford it for everyone, remain open questions.", q:"According to the passage, participants in the Finnish trial ----.", o:["stopped looking for work once the payments began","received the payment for the rest of their lives","were chosen from among all working citizens","had to prove that they were looking for a job","were no less likely to find work than people in a comparison group"], a:4, e:"“were, if anything, slightly more likely to find jobs than a comparison group” ifadesi, iş bulma olasılıklarının daha düşük olmadığını söyleyen E ile karşılanır. A eleştirmenlerin korkusudur ve bulgularla çelişir; D ise “without conditions” ile çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-24", sub:"rd-detail", from:["reading-05","w6-10"], passage:"Fears that machines will destroy jobs are almost as old as industrialisation itself. In the early nineteenth century, English textile workers known as Luddites smashed the new mechanical looms they blamed for their falling wages. In the long run, their fears proved mistaken for the economy as a whole: mechanisation lowered the price of cloth, demand soared, and far more people eventually worked in the textile industry than before. Economists often cite this history to argue that technology creates at least as many jobs as it destroys. Yet the argument has limits. The new jobs did not necessarily go to the workers who had lost their old ones, and for many families the transition brought decades of hardship. Today's debates about artificial intelligence may need to take both lessons into account.", q:"According to the passage, mechanisation in the textile industry ----.", o:["was introduced after the Luddites' wages had begun to rise","reduced the demand for cloth considerably","eventually increased employment in the industry by making cloth cheaper and more in demand","gave most of the new jobs to the Luddites themselves","ended the hardship of textile workers' families at once"], a:2, e:"“mechanisation lowered the price of cloth, demand soared, and far more people eventually worked in the textile industry” cümlesi C şıkkında yeniden yazılmıştır. D metinle çelişir; yeni işler kaybedenlere gitmek zorunda değildi."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-25", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-04"], passage:"Tardigrades, microscopic animals less than a millimetre long, are famous for their extraordinary toughness. When their environment dries out, they curl up, lose almost all the water in their bodies and enter a state in which their metabolism nearly stops. In this condition, they have survived temperatures close to absolute zero, intense radiation and even exposure to the vacuum of space. Researchers have identified a special protein that appears to protect their DNA from damage. This discovery has attracted interest beyond biology: scientists are exploring whether similar molecules could help to preserve vaccines without refrigeration or protect human cells during radiation therapy. Such applications remain experimental, but they show how the study of obscure organisms can lead to practical benefits.", q:"The word “obscure” in the passage is closest in meaning to ----.", o:["dangerous","microscopic","little-known","ancient","complex"], a:2, e:"“obscure organisms”, çok az tanınan ve bilim dışında ilgi görmeyen canlılar anlamındadır; bu yüzden “little-known” doğrudur. B tardigradlar gerçekten mikroskobik olduğu için çekicidir ama kelimenin anlamı bu değildir."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-26", sub:"rd-detail", from:["w6-02","reading-02"], passage:"Of the roughly seven thousand languages spoken today, linguists estimate that around half may disappear by the end of this century. Most are spoken by small communities whose children increasingly grow up using a national or global language instead. The loss matters for more than sentimental reasons. Each language encodes knowledge accumulated over generations, including detailed names for local plants, animals and medicines that may exist nowhere else. Revival is difficult but not impossible. Hebrew, which for centuries was used mainly in religious and scholarly texts, became the everyday language of millions in the twentieth century, and communities from Wales to New Zealand have increased the number of young speakers through schools that teach entirely in the endangered language.", q:"According to the passage, the main reason why many small languages are in danger is that ----.", o:["linguists have failed to record them in time","younger generations are adopting more widely spoken languages","their speakers have moved to Wales and New Zealand","they contain no words for local plants and animals","they are now used only in religious texts"], a:1, e:"“children increasingly grow up using a national or global language instead” ifadesi B şıkkında yeniden yazılmıştır. E İbranicenin geçmişteki durumuna ait bir detayı yanlış yere taşır."},
  {id:"r-rd-detail-27", sub:"rd-detail", from:["reading-05","w6-06"], passage:"Until the middle of the nineteenth century, most towns set their clocks by the position of the sun, so that noon in one town might be several minutes earlier or later than noon in a town to the east or west. This caused few problems when travel was slow. The arrival of the railways changed that. Timetables became confusing when every station kept its own time, and mistakes could lead to accidents. In Britain, railway companies adopted a single standard time based on Greenwich in the 1840s, and most towns soon followed, although the new time was not made legally binding until 1880. In 1884, an international conference established Greenwich as the starting point for a worldwide system of time zones.", q:"According to the passage, the standard time adopted by British railway companies in the 1840s ----.", o:["was made compulsory by law immediately","was taken up by most towns well before it became a legal requirement","was based on the local time of each station","was rejected by the international conference of 1884","caused a large number of railway accidents"], a:1, e:"Kasabaların çoğu yeni saati kısa sürede benimsemiş, saat ise ancak 1880'de yasal olarak bağlayıcı olmuştur; B bunu verir. A “not made legally binding until 1880” ile çelişir; C standart saatten önceki duruma aittir."},

  /* ---------- rd-inference ---------- */
  {id:"r-rd-inference-01", sub:"rd-inference", from:["reading-03","w6-07"], passage:"Microplastics, fragments of plastic smaller than five millimetres, have been detected almost everywhere scientists have looked, from Arctic snow to human blood. Their presence in bottled and tap water has understandably alarmed the public. However, researchers stress that detection is not the same as harm. Most studies so far have exposed cells or animals to concentrations far higher than those people normally encounter, so their relevance to human health remains unclear. A further difficulty is that laboratories use different methods to count particles, which makes results hard to compare. The World Health Organization has therefore called not for panic but for standardised measurement techniques and long-term studies, while recommending that plastic pollution be reduced regardless of the outcome.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that the World Health Organization ----.", o:["has confirmed that microplastics in drinking water pose a serious risk to health","believes that current laboratory methods for counting particles are fully reliable","considers reducing plastic pollution worthwhile even if microplastics turn out to be harmless to health","advises people to stop drinking bottled water immediately","regards studies using very high concentrations as the most relevant to human health"], a:2, e:"DSÖ plastik kirliliğinin “regardless of the outcome” (sonuç ne olursa olsun) azaltılmasını önerdiğine göre, zararsız çıksa bile azaltmayı değerli bulur. A çekicidir ama metin sağlık etkisinin “unclear” olduğunu söyler; DSÖ panik değil ölçüm standardı ister."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-02", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-12","reading-03"], passage:"Johannes Gutenberg's printing press, developed around 1450, is often credited with transforming European society almost overnight. The reality was more gradual. For several decades, printed books remained expensive, and most were Latin religious texts aimed at a small educated elite. The press began to have wider effects only when printers turned to cheaper pamphlets written in local languages. During the Reformation, Martin Luther's short tracts were reprinted so quickly that his ideas reached readers across Germany within weeks, something no handwritten copy could have achieved. Historians therefore argue that the technology alone did not change society; its impact depended on how it was used and on readers' demand for new kinds of texts.", q:"The example of Luther's tracts is given in the passage to illustrate ----.", o:["why printed books remained expensive for several decades","how printing in local languages could spread ideas far faster than copying by hand","that Luther was opposed to the use of Latin in religious texts","the reasons why Gutenberg developed the printing press","how the educated elite controlled the production of pamphlets"], a:1, e:"Luther örneği, matbaanın yerel dilde ucuz broşürlere yönelince geniş etki yarattığı cümlesinin hemen ardından gelir ve “no handwritten copy could have achieved” diyerek hızı vurgular. C metinde yer almayan bir çıkarımdır; Luther'in Latinceye karşı tutumundan söz edilmez."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-03", sub:"rd-inference", from:["reading-03","w6-11"], passage:"For decades, most economists taught that raising the minimum wage would inevitably cost jobs: if labour becomes more expensive, employers will hire fewer workers. In the early 1990s, two economists challenged this view by comparing fast-food restaurants in two neighbouring American states, only one of which had raised its minimum wage. Contrary to expectations, employment in the state with the higher wage did not fall. Their study provoked fierce debate, and later research has produced mixed results. A growing consensus now holds that moderate increases have little effect on employment, while very large increases, particularly in poorer regions, may indeed reduce hiring. The question, in other words, is less whether a minimum wage harms jobs than at what level it begins to do so.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that, according to the current consensus, ----.", o:["any increase in the minimum wage will inevitably cost jobs","the study from the early 1990s has been shown to be entirely wrong","poorer regions should not have a minimum wage at all","the effect of a minimum wage increase on jobs depends partly on its size and on local economic conditions","economists no longer disagree about any aspect of the minimum wage"], a:3, e:"Ilımlı artışların etkisi az, yoksul bölgelerdeki çok büyük artışların etkisi olabileceğine göre sonuç artışın büyüklüğüne ve bölgeye bağlıdır. A metnin eski ve terk edilen görüşüdür; C “may reduce hiring” ifadesini aşırı bir politika önerisine çevirir."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-04", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-07","reading-03"], passage:"The trillions of bacteria living in the human gut were long thought to matter mainly for digestion. Over the past two decades, however, researchers have found that these microbes also communicate with the brain, producing chemicals that can influence mood and stress. In one striking experiment, mice raised without any gut bacteria reacted more strongly to stress than normal mice, and their behaviour changed when they received bacteria from other animals. Such findings have led to excitement about so-called psychobiotics, probiotic supplements that might one day treat anxiety or depression. Most researchers urge caution, though. Results in mice do not always apply to humans, and the few human trials conducted so far have been small and inconsistent.", q:"It can be understood from the passage that most researchers ----.", o:["have already approved psychobiotics as a treatment for anxiety","believe that gut bacteria have no effect on the brain","regard psychobiotics as promising but not yet proven to work in humans","consider further human trials to be unnecessary","think that results obtained in mice are of no relevance to human research"], a:2, e:"Araştırmacılar heyecanı paylaşmakla birlikte insan denemelerinin küçük ve tutarsız olduğunu söyleyerek temkin ister; bu, “umut verici ama kanıtlanmamış” demektir. E aşırıdır; metin fare sonuçlarının insanlara “her zaman” uygulanamadığını söyler, hiç ilgisiz olduğunu değil."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-05", sub:"rd-inference", from:["reading-03","w6-03"], passage:"Artificial light at night is one of the fastest-growing forms of pollution, yet it receives far less attention than air or water pollution. Its effects on wildlife can be severe. Many nocturnal insects are drawn to street lamps, where they circle until they die of exhaustion or are eaten, and some scientists believe this contributes to the widely reported decline in insect numbers. Migrating birds, which use the stars to navigate, can become disoriented by brightly lit buildings and collide with them. Unlike many environmental problems, however, light pollution is relatively easy to reduce. Shielding lamps so that they point downwards, switching to warmer colours and dimming lights late at night can cut its impact considerably at little cost.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that ----.", o:["artificial light may be one of several factors behind the fall in insect numbers","light pollution has been proven to be the main cause of insect decline","birds that migrate at night rely only on the Moon to navigate","most cities have already switched to warmer and dimmer street lights","reducing light pollution requires expensive new technology"], a:0, e:"Metin bazı bilim insanlarının ışığın böcek azalmasına “katkıda bulunduğuna” (contributes) inandığını söyler; yani olası etkenlerden biridir. B temkinli “some scientists believe … contributes” ifadesini kesin ve tek neden iddiasına çevirir."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-06", sub:"rd-inference", from:["reading-06"], passage:"In many countries, a large proportion of all antibiotics sold is given not to people but to farm animals. For decades, farmers added small doses to animal feed, not to treat illness but because the drugs made animals grow faster. Scientists have long warned that this practice encourages the spread of resistant bacteria, which can pass to humans through food, water or direct contact. In 2006, the European Union banned the use of antibiotics for growth promotion. Denmark, which had introduced similar restrictions even earlier, offers an instructive example: its pig industry continued to expand after the ban, and levels of resistance in farm animals fell. Critics had predicted serious economic losses, but these largely failed to materialise.", q:"The author's attitude towards the ban on antibiotics for growth promotion can best be described as ----.", o:["approving","disapproving","indifferent","uncertain","ironic"], a:0, e:"Yazar Danimarka'yı “instructive example” olarak sunar, direncin düştüğünü ve eleştirmenlerin öngördüğü kayıpların “largely failed to materialise” olduğunu vurgular; tutumu onaylayıcıdır. Eleştirilere yer verilmesi B şıkkını çekici kılar ama yazar bu eleştiriyi çürütür."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-07", sub:"rd-inference", from:["reading-03","w6-03"], passage:"When the pandemic forced millions of office workers to work from home, many commentators predicted the end of the big city. If people no longer needed to commute, why would they pay high rents in crowded urban centres? Some did leave: in several countries, house prices in small towns and rural areas rose sharply as city dwellers moved out. Yet the exodus proved smaller than expected. Most people who left moved only to suburbs within reach of the city, and many companies have since required staff to return to the office for at least part of the week. Cities, it seems, offer more than office space; restaurants, cultural life and the chance to meet new people continue to attract residents.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that ----.", o:["office space in city centres will soon disappear entirely","most companies now allow their staff to work from home permanently","house prices in large cities fell sharply during the pandemic","restaurants and cultural venues have moved to small towns","the attraction of cities does not depend only on the jobs located there"], a:4, e:"Son cümle şehirlerin ofis alanından fazlasını (restoran, kültür hayatı, yeni insanlarla tanışma) sunduğunu söyler; demek ki şehrin çekiciliği yalnızca işlere bağlı değildir. B metinle çelişir; şirketlerin çoğu çalışanları haftanın bir kısmı ofise dönmeye zorlamıştır."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-08", sub:"rd-inference", from:["reading-06","w6-07"], passage:"For much of the twentieth century, bilingualism was considered a disadvantage for children, who were thought to become confused by learning two languages at once. From the 1960s onwards, this view was reversed, and numerous studies reported that bilingual people outperformed monolinguals on tasks requiring attention and self-control. More recently, however, the so-called bilingual advantage has itself come under scrutiny. Large studies with thousands of participants have often failed to replicate it, and some researchers suspect that journals were more willing to publish positive results than negative ones. None of this means that learning a second language is not worthwhile; its social, cultural and economic benefits are not in doubt. What is disputed is only whether it also sharpens general thinking skills.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that the author ----.", o:["believes that bilingualism harms children's development","thinks that the studies from the 1960s were deliberately falsified","considers the social benefits of bilingualism to be doubtful","would still recommend learning a second language, whatever the outcome of the debate","is certain that bilingualism sharpens general thinking skills"], a:3, e:"Yazar ikinci dilin sosyal, kültürel ve ekonomik faydalarının “not in doubt” olduğunu ve tartışmanın yalnızca düşünme becerileriyle ilgili olduğunu söyler; bu yüzden tartışmanın sonucundan bağımsız olarak dil öğrenmeyi değerli bulur. C bu cümlenin tam tersidir; B ise yayın yanlılığı şüphesini sahtekârlık iddiasına çevirir."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-09", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-07","w6-08"], passage:"Since the mid-2000s, beekeepers in North America and Europe have reported alarming losses of honeybee colonies, sometimes more than a third in a single winter. Early media coverage often blamed a single culprit, whether mobile phone signals or a particular group of pesticides. Scientists now agree that the picture is more complex. Parasitic mites, viral diseases, poor nutrition caused by the loss of wildflowers and exposure to pesticides all weaken colonies, and these stresses appear to reinforce one another. A colony that is already undernourished, for example, is less able to resist infection. For this reason, researchers argue that no single ban or treatment will solve the problem; only measures that address several factors at once are likely to succeed.", q:"It can be understood from the passage that early media reports on the loss of bee colonies ----.", o:["oversimplified the causes of the problem","were later confirmed by scientific research","ignored the possible role of pesticides completely","were written mainly by beekeepers","exaggerated the number of colonies that had been lost"], a:0, e:"Medya tek bir suçlu gösterirken bilim insanları “the picture is more complex” der; yani haberler nedenleri basite indirgemiştir. C yanlıştır; medyanın suçladığı şeylerden biri zaten pestisitlerdi."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-10", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-08","reading-03"], passage:"Many Roman harbour walls and buildings have survived for two thousand years, while modern concrete structures often begin to crumble within decades. Engineers long assumed that the Romans' secret lay in the volcanic ash they mixed into their concrete. Recent research suggests that this is only part of the answer. Analysis of samples has revealed small white lumps of lime that were once dismissed as a sign of careless mixing. In fact, these lumps appear to allow the material to repair itself: when water enters a crack, it dissolves some of the lime, which then hardens and seals the gap. Researchers hope that similar self-healing mixtures could extend the lifespan of modern buildings and reduce the large carbon emissions associated with cement production.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that engineers' earlier explanation of the strength of Roman concrete ----.", o:["has now been completely rejected","was based on an analysis of the white lumps of lime","ignored the role of volcanic ash","was first proposed by the Romans themselves","was incomplete rather than entirely wrong"], a:4, e:"Araştırmalar volkanik kül açıklamasının “only part of the answer” olduğunu söyler; yani eski açıklama yanlış değil, eksiktir. A bu kısmi düzeltmeyi tam bir reddetmeye çevirir; C ise eski açıklamanın tam da volkanik küle dayandığını gözden kaçırır."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-11", sub:"rd-inference", from:["reading-06","w6-07"], passage:"When microfinance emerged in Bangladesh in the 1970s, offering small loans to poor people who had no access to banks, it was hailed as a powerful weapon against poverty. Its founder later received the Nobel Peace Prize, and lenders spread across the developing world. By the 2010s, however, a series of carefully designed studies had tempered this enthusiasm. In six countries, researchers found that access to microloans helped some borrowers expand their businesses but did not, on average, raise household incomes significantly or lift families out of poverty. This does not make microfinance useless: it gives households more flexibility in managing money and coping with emergencies. It simply appears to be a modest tool rather than the revolution its supporters once promised.", q:"The author's attitude towards microfinance can best be described as ----.", o:["enthusiastic","balanced","hostile","indifferent","mocking"], a:1, e:"Yazar mikrofinansın devrim olmadığını kabul eder ama “does not make microfinance useless” diyerek faydalarını da sayar; tutumu dengelidir. A metnin başında aktarılan ilk coşkuyu yansıtır, yazarın kendi görüşünü değil."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-12", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-11","reading-03"], passage:"Cultivated meat is produced by growing animal cells in large steel tanks rather than by raising and slaughtering animals. Its supporters claim it could dramatically reduce the land, water and greenhouse gas emissions associated with livestock farming. In 2020, Singapore became the first country to allow its sale. However, significant obstacles remain. The nutrient solution in which the cells grow is expensive, and producing meat at scale requires vast amounts of energy. One recent analysis even suggested that, if that energy comes from fossil fuels, cultivated meat could have a larger carbon footprint than some conventional beef. Whether the technology fulfils its environmental promise may therefore depend less on laboratory breakthroughs than on the supply of clean electricity.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that cultivated meat ----.", o:["always has a smaller carbon footprint than conventional beef","has already replaced livestock farming in Singapore","would be more environmentally attractive in a country with plenty of renewable electricity","will become cheap as soon as laboratory techniques improve","uses less energy than any other method of food production"], a:2, e:"Enerji fosil yakıttan gelirse karbon ayak izi bazı sığır etlerinden bile büyük olabileceğine göre, temiz elektriğin bol olduğu yerde çevresel açıdan daha avantajlıdır. A “always” ile aşırı genelleme yapar ve metindeki analizle çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-13", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-08","reading-03"], passage:"The Black Death, which killed perhaps a third of Europe's population between 1347 and 1351, was a catastrophe of almost unimaginable scale. Paradoxically, it also improved the lives of many survivors. With so many workers dead, labour became scarce, and peasants in England and elsewhere were able to demand higher wages or leave their lords in search of better conditions. Landowners and governments tried to resist: England passed laws fixing wages at pre-plague levels. These laws proved largely ineffective, however, and real wages for labourers rose substantially over the following century. Some historians argue that this shift weakened the feudal system and, in the long term, helped to prepare the ground for the economic changes of early modern Europe.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that before the Black Death, ----.", o:["the large supply of labour kept peasants' bargaining power weak","peasants earned higher real wages than they did in the following century","landowners were unable to find enough workers","the feudal system had already disappeared in England","most people in Europe lived in large cities"], a:0, e:"Veba sonrası işgücü kıtlaşınca köylüler yüksek ücret talep edebildiğine göre, öncesinde bol işgücü pazarlık gücünü zayıf tutuyordu. B tam tersidir; reel ücretler vebadan sonraki yüzyılda artmıştır. C ise veba sonrası duruma aittir."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-14", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-11","w6-07"], passage:"The deep ocean floor is scattered with potato-sized nodules rich in cobalt, nickel and manganese, metals that are essential for the batteries of electric cars. Mining companies argue that collecting them would cause less damage than mining on land, where forests are cleared and local communities displaced. Marine scientists are far from convinced. The deep sea is among the least explored environments on Earth; many of the animals living on the nodules have not yet been described, and some areas disturbed in experiments decades ago still show little sign of recovery. Several governments and car manufacturers have therefore called for a pause in deep-sea mining until its environmental effects are better understood.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that the call for a pause in deep-sea mining is based mainly on ----.", o:["the high cost of collecting nodules from the ocean floor","the lack of knowledge about how mining would affect deep-sea ecosystems","the damage caused by mining on land","a fall in the demand for electric cars","evidence that the nodules contain little valuable metal"], a:1, e:"Ara verme çağrısı “until its environmental effects are better understood” koşuluna bağlanmıştır; gerekçe derin deniz hakkındaki bilgi eksikliğidir. C maden şirketlerinin derin deniz madenciliğini savunmak için kullandığı argümandır."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-15", sub:"rd-inference", from:["reading-03","w6-03"], passage:"Many university students now take notes on laptops, which allow them to type far faster than they can write. It might seem obvious that recording more of a lecture would lead to better learning. A widely cited series of experiments suggested the opposite: students who took notes by hand performed better on conceptual questions than those who typed. The researchers argued that because writing is slow, handwriters are forced to summarise and rephrase ideas, whereas typists tend to copy lectures word for word without processing them. Later attempts to repeat the experiments, however, have produced weaker and less consistent results. The safest conclusion may be that what matters is not the tool but whether students actively think about the material as they record it.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that ----.", o:["later studies found that typing is clearly the better method","universities have stopped allowing students to use laptops","the original researchers invented their results","the advantage of handwritten notes is less certain than the original experiments suggested","writing quickly always improves understanding"], a:3, e:"Tekrar denemeleri “weaker and less consistent results” verdiğine göre el yazısının avantajı ilk deneylerin gösterdiği kadar kesin değildir. A çekicidir ama metin sonuçların zayıfladığını söyler, tersine döndüğünü değil."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-16", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-08","reading-03"], passage:"In April 1815, Mount Tambora in present-day Indonesia erupted in the most powerful volcanic explosion in recorded history. Apart from killing tens of thousands of people locally, it threw so much ash and sulphur into the upper atmosphere that sunlight was partially blocked across the globe. The following year became known in Europe and North America as 'the year without a summer'. Snow fell in New England in June, crops failed across much of Europe, and food prices soared, leading to famine and riots. Yet people at the time had no idea that the strange weather was linked to an eruption on the other side of the world; this connection was established by scientists only in the twentieth century.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that people in Europe in 1816 ----.", o:["had predicted the famine long in advance","did not realise that their failed harvests had a volcanic cause","blamed the eruption for the unusual weather","benefited from lower food prices","were unaffected by the eruption"], a:1, e:"İnsanlar tuhaf havanın patlamayla ilgili olduğunu bilmiyordu ve bağlantı ancak 20. yüzyılda kuruldu; öyleyse kıtlığın volkanik nedenini fark etmemişlerdir. C bu bilgiyle doğrudan çelişir; D ise “food prices soared” ile çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-17", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-12"], passage:"Traditional economic theory assumed that people weigh gains and losses of the same size equally. Research in behavioural economics has shown that this is not the case. In numerous experiments, people feel the pain of losing a sum of money roughly twice as strongly as the pleasure of gaining the same amount, a tendency known as loss aversion. This helps to explain behaviour that otherwise seems irrational, such as investors' reluctance to sell shares that have fallen in value, since doing so means admitting a loss. Governments have also put the idea to use. In one trial, teachers who received a bonus in advance and were told they would have to return it if their pupils' results did not improve achieved better results than teachers promised the same bonus at the end of the year.", q:"The example of the teachers' bonus is given in the passage to illustrate ----.", o:["why teachers are paid less than other professionals","that bonuses paid at the end of the year are the most effective kind","the irrational behaviour of investors on the stock market","the difficulty of measuring pupils' results accurately","how the fear of losing something can be used to improve people's performance"], a:4, e:"Örnek “Governments have also put the idea to use” cümlesinin ardından gelir: ikramiyeyi geri verme korkusu, yani kayıptan kaçınma, öğretmenlerin başarısını artırmıştır. B tersidir; yıl sonunda ikramiye vaat edilen öğretmenler daha kötü sonuç almıştır."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-18", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-11","reading-03"], passage:"For much of the twentieth century, land managers in Australia and North America treated all wildfires as enemies to be put out as quickly as possible. This approach had an unintended consequence: dead wood and dry vegetation built up in forests, so that when fires did break out, they burned far more intensely. Indigenous peoples in both regions had long followed a different practice, setting small, cool fires in the right seasons to clear undergrowth and create a patchwork of burned and unburned land. After a series of devastating fire seasons, governments have begun to hire Indigenous rangers and adopt similar methods. Although controlled burning cannot prevent every disaster, especially as the climate grows hotter and drier, it can significantly reduce the fuel available to large fires.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that the small fires set by Indigenous peoples ----.", o:["were the main cause of the devastating fire seasons","were illegal in both Australia and North America","burned more intensely than modern wildfires","will be able to prevent every disaster once they are widely adopted","limited the amount of fuel available to more destructive fires"], a:4, e:"Küçük ve serin yangınlar çalılığı temizlediğine ve kontrollü yakma “reduce the fuel available to large fires” dendiğine göre, bu uygulama büyük yangınların yakıtını sınırlamıştır. D metnin “cannot prevent every disaster” uyarısını yok sayar."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-19", sub:"rd-inference", from:["reading-03","w6-08"], passage:"Gene-editing techniques such as CRISPR allow scientists to make precise changes to a plant's own DNA, for example switching off a gene that makes wheat vulnerable to a fungal disease. Unlike earlier genetically modified crops, edited plants often contain no genes from other species, and the changes they carry could in principle have arisen through natural mutation or conventional breeding. For this reason, several countries, including Argentina and Japan, regulate them much like traditionally bred varieties. The European Union initially took the opposite view, ruling in 2018 that edited crops should face the same strict rules as other genetically modified organisms. Many European scientists criticised the decision, warning that it would push research abroad, and the EU has since moved to relax the rules for some edited plants.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that in Argentina and Japan, ----.", o:["a gene-edited crop without foreign genes faces fewer regulatory obstacles than it would have under the EU's 2018 ruling","gene editing of crops has been banned since 2018","all genetically modified organisms are regulated in the same way as traditional varieties","scientists have moved their research to Europe","wheat is no longer affected by fungal diseases"], a:0, e:"Bu ülkeler düzenlenmiş bitkileri geleneksel çeşitler gibi, AB ise 2018'de katı GDO kuralları gibi düzenlediğine göre, Arjantin ve Japonya'da daha az engel vardır. C aşırı genellemedir; metin yalnızca gen düzenlemeli bitkilerden söz eder, tüm GDO'lardan değil."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-20", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-07","reading-03"], passage:"Loneliness is usually thought of as an emotional problem rather than a medical one. A growing body of research, however, suggests that it can have serious consequences for physical health. Large studies following people over many years have linked chronic loneliness and social isolation to higher rates of heart disease, stroke and dementia, and to a risk of early death comparable to that of well-known factors such as obesity. Researchers believe that long-term loneliness keeps the body in a state of stress, raising blood pressure and weakening the immune system. In response, the United Kingdom appointed a minister for loneliness in 2018, and some doctors now practise 'social prescribing', referring isolated patients to community groups instead of, or as well as, prescribing medication.", q:"It can be understood from the passage that doctors who practise social prescribing ----.", o:["refuse to prescribe medication to lonely patients","work under the direction of the minister for loneliness","believe that loneliness has no physical effects","treat only patients who are suffering from dementia","regard social contact as something that can contribute to their patients' health"], a:4, e:"Bu doktorlar yalnız hastaları ilaca ek olarak ya da onun yerine topluluk gruplarına yönlendirdiğine göre sosyal teması sağlığa katkı sağlayan bir unsur olarak görürler. A “or as well as” ifadesini gözden kaçırır; ilaç her zaman reddedilmez."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-21", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-08","reading-03"], passage:"Easter Island, famous for its giant stone statues, has often been presented as a warning about environmental self-destruction. According to this popular account, the islanders cut down all their trees to move the statues, causing soil erosion, famine and a violent collapse of their society. Recent research has challenged much of this story. Radiocarbon dating suggests that the island was settled later than once thought, and rats introduced by the first settlers may have destroyed the forest by eating palm seeds. Moreover, studies of ancient farming sites indicate that the population remained relatively stable until Europeans arrived in 1722, bringing diseases and, later, slave raids that devastated the community. Some researchers now see the islanders as an example of resilience rather than recklessness.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that, according to recent research, the sharp decline in the island's population ----.", o:["was caused by famine resulting from soil erosion","took place before the first statues were built","was the result of wars among the islanders","followed contact with Europeans rather than preceding it","occurred soon after the island was first settled"], a:3, e:"Nüfus 1722'de Avrupalılar gelene kadar görece sabit kalmış, ardından hastalıklar ve köle baskınları topluluğu yıkmıştır; demek ki düşüş temastan sonra gelmiştir. A ve C popüler anlatının unsurlarıdır, yeni araştırmaların değil."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-22", sub:"rd-inference", from:["reading-06"], passage:"In recent years, popular books and documentaries have described forests as cooperative communities in which trees 'talk' to one another through underground fungal networks, sharing sugar with their offspring and warning neighbours of insect attacks. The idea is appealing, and it rests on real discoveries: fungi do connect the roots of many trees, and nutrients can move between them. However, a recent review of the evidence concluded that many of the most striking claims go well beyond what field studies have shown. Several influential experiments involved small numbers of seedlings, and the amounts of carbon transferred were often too small to be of clear benefit. The reviewers did not deny that fungal networks exist; they argued that their role has been exaggerated in public discussion.", q:"The author's attitude towards the popular image of forests as cooperative communities can best be described as ----.", o:["wholly accepting","indifferent","sceptical","enthusiastic","hostile"], a:2, e:"Yazar fikrin “appealing” olduğunu ve gerçek bulgulara dayandığını kabul eder, ama “However” ile iddiaların abartıldığını vurgular; tutumu şüphecidir. E çok güçlüdür; yazar mantar ağlarının varlığını reddetmez, yalnızca abartıyı eleştirir."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-23", sub:"rd-inference", from:["reading-03","w6-03"], passage:"Universal basic income, a regular cash payment given to every citizen regardless of whether they work, has attracted supporters across the political spectrum. Critics have long objected that such payments would discourage people from working. Evidence from trials offers some reassurance on this point. In Finland, two thousand unemployed people received a monthly payment without conditions between 2017 and 2018; they reported greater well-being and were, if anything, slightly more likely to find jobs than a comparison group. Nevertheless, the trials have been small and temporary, and participants knew the payments would end. Whether people would behave in the same way if they received the money for life, and how governments could afford it for everyone, remain open questions.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that the results of the existing trials ----.", o:["may not reliably predict how people would behave under a permanent scheme","prove that a basic income would be affordable for all governments","confirm the critics' fears about the effect on work","were ignored by the Finnish government","apply equally to employed and unemployed people"], a:0, e:"Denemeler küçük ve geçiciydi, katılımcılar ödemenin biteceğini biliyordu; bu yüzden sonuçlar kalıcı bir sistemi güvenilir biçimde öngörmeyebilir. C tersidir; deneyler eleştirmenlerin kaygısı konusunda “reassurance” sağlamıştır."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-24", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-07","reading-06"], passage:"Fears that machines will destroy jobs are almost as old as industrialisation itself. In the early nineteenth century, English textile workers known as Luddites smashed the new mechanical looms they blamed for their falling wages. In the long run, their fears proved mistaken for the economy as a whole: mechanisation lowered the price of cloth, demand soared, and far more people eventually worked in the textile industry than before. Economists often cite this history to argue that technology creates at least as many jobs as it destroys. Yet the argument has limits. The new jobs did not necessarily go to the workers who had lost their old ones, and for many families the transition brought decades of hardship. Today's debates about artificial intelligence may need to take both lessons into account.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that the author ----.", o:["sympathises with the Luddites' decision to destroy the machines","believes that artificial intelligence should be banned","rejects the idea that technology can create new jobs","considers the history of the textile industry irrelevant to modern debates","accepts the economists' argument in general but thinks it overlooks the experience of individual workers"], a:4, e:"Yazar ekonomistlerin argümanını büyük ölçüde doğru bulur ama “Yet the argument has limits” diyerek yeni işlerin kaybedenlere gitmediğini vurgular. D yanlıştır; yazar yapay zekâ tartışmasının “both lessons” dikkate alması gerektiğini söyler."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-25", sub:"rd-inference", from:["reading-03","w6-03"], passage:"Tardigrades, microscopic animals less than a millimetre long, are famous for their extraordinary toughness. When their environment dries out, they curl up, lose almost all the water in their bodies and enter a state in which their metabolism nearly stops. In this condition, they have survived temperatures close to absolute zero, intense radiation and even exposure to the vacuum of space. Researchers have identified a special protein that appears to protect their DNA from damage. This discovery has attracted interest beyond biology: scientists are exploring whether similar molecules could help to preserve vaccines without refrigeration or protect human cells during radiation therapy. Such applications remain experimental, but they show how the study of obscure organisms can lead to practical benefits.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that the protein identified in tardigrades ----.", o:["already allows vaccines to be stored without refrigeration","is found in all living organisms","has not yet been used successfully in routine medical treatment","causes tardigrades to lose water from their bodies","was first discovered during an experiment in space"], a:2, e:"Bilim insanları benzer moleküllerin aşılarda ya da radyoterapide işe yarayıp yaramayacağını “araştırıyor” ve uygulamalar “remain experimental”; demek ki henüz rutin tedavide kullanılmıyor. A temkinli “exploring whether” ifadesini gerçekleşmiş bir sonuca çevirir."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-26", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-12"], passage:"Of the roughly seven thousand languages spoken today, linguists estimate that around half may disappear by the end of this century. Most are spoken by small communities whose children increasingly grow up using a national or global language instead. The loss matters for more than sentimental reasons. Each language encodes knowledge accumulated over generations, including detailed names for local plants, animals and medicines that may exist nowhere else. Revival is difficult but not impossible. Hebrew, which for centuries was used mainly in religious and scholarly texts, became the everyday language of millions in the twentieth century, and communities from Wales to New Zealand have increased the number of young speakers through schools that teach entirely in the endangered language.", q:"The example of Hebrew is given in the passage to illustrate ----.", o:["why religious texts are difficult to translate","that a language no longer used in daily life can be restored as an everyday language","how national languages gradually replace smaller ones","that language revival is only possible in small communities","the knowledge of medicines contained in ancient languages"], a:1, e:"Örnek “Revival is difficult but not impossible” cümlesinin hemen ardından gelir: yüzyıllarca gündelik hayatta kullanılmayan İbranice milyonların günlük dili olmuştur. D yanlıştır; İbranice milyonlarca kişinin dili hâline gelmiştir."},
  {id:"r-rd-inference-27", sub:"rd-inference", from:["w6-08"], passage:"Until the middle of the nineteenth century, most towns set their clocks by the position of the sun, so that noon in one town might be several minutes earlier or later than noon in a town to the east or west. This caused few problems when travel was slow. The arrival of the railways changed that. Timetables became confusing when every station kept its own time, and mistakes could lead to accidents. In Britain, railway companies adopted a single standard time based on Greenwich in the 1840s, and most towns soon followed, although the new time was not made legally binding until 1880. In 1884, an international conference established Greenwich as the starting point for a worldwide system of time zones.", q:"It can be inferred from the passage that before the railways, ----.", o:["all towns in Britain already used Greenwich time","timetables were the main cause of travel accidents","travellers frequently complained about differences in local time","differences in local time between towns were of little practical importance","noon occurred at exactly the same moment everywhere"], a:3, e:"Yavaş seyahat edilirken yerel saat farkları “few problems” yaratıyordu; demek ki pratikte pek önemli değildi. C bu ifadeyle çelişir; E ise kasabalar arasında dakikalarca fark olduğunu söyleyen metne ters düşer."},

  /* ---------- rd-dialogue ---------- */
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-01", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["reading-07"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nStudent: Our textbook says acid rain was a major problem in Europe in the 1980s. Why don't we hear about it any more?\nProfessor: Mainly because governments forced power stations to cut their sulphur emissions, often by fitting filters to their chimneys.\nStudent: ----\nProfessor: They will, but slowly. Some lakes that became too acidic for fish are still recovering decades later.\nStudent: So the damage has outlasted the pollution itself.", o:["Why did power stations increase their sulphur emissions?","How much did it cost to fit the filters to the chimneys?","So will the lakes and forests that were damaged eventually recover completely?","Did the fish in those lakes cause the acid rain?","Isn't acid rain mainly caused by car exhaust?"], a:2, e:"Profesörün “They will, but slowly” cevabı “will” ile sorulmuş ve iyileşmeyle ilgili bir soru gerektirir; ardından göllerin hâlâ toparlandığı söylenir. E evet/hayır sorusudur ama “They will” cevabıyla dilbilgisel ve anlamsal olarak bağlanmaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-02", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["reading-08"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nInterviewer: Your new study suggests that video games can improve certain mental skills. Won't that surprise many parents?\nPsychologist: Probably. The players in our study showed faster reactions and better visual attention than non-players.\nInterviewer: ----\nPsychologist: Not at all. Those benefits came from a limited number of action games, and excessive playing still harms sleep and schoolwork.\nInterviewer: So moderation is still the key.", o:["Which game did your participants enjoy most?","How long did the study take to complete?","Do you play video games yourself?","So are you recommending that children should play as much as they like?","Why do non-players have faster reactions than players?"], a:3, e:"“Not at all” cevabı ve ardından gelen “excessive playing still harms …” açıklaması, çocukların istedikleri kadar oynaması gerektiğini mi söylediğini soran D şıkkına uyar. C de evet/hayır sorusudur ama cevabın devamıyla anlamca bağlanmaz; E ise çalışmanın bulgusunu tersine çevirir."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-03", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["reading-09"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nCustomer: I bought this houseplant only a month ago, but its leaves have already started turning yellow.\nGardener: Yellow leaves are often a sign of too much water rather than too little. The roots can rot if the soil never dries out.\nCustomer: ----\nGardener: That would explain it. Let the soil dry out for a week, and make sure the pot has holes in the bottom.\nCustomer: Thanks, I'll try that.", o:["Should I move it to a sunnier window, then?","How often should I add fertiliser to the soil?","Is it true that some plants never need any water?","Oh dear, I've been watering it every single day in case it got thirsty.","Actually, I only water it about once a month."], a:3, e:"“That would explain it” bir açıklamaya/itirafa verilen tepkidir: her gün sulamak aşırı suyu ve kök çürümesini açıklar. E de bir sulama bilgisi verir ama az sulama, aşırı su açıklamasını desteklemez; A ise bir sorudur ve “That would explain it” ile bağlanmaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-04", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-01"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nDeniz: I read that the number of wolves in Yellowstone has grown steadily since they were reintroduced in 1995.\nKaan: ----\nDeniz: True, and scientists say they have even changed the landscape by keeping elk away from riverbanks, which has allowed trees to grow back.\nKaan: So a single predator can affect a whole ecosystem.", o:["I've never understood why people visit national parks.","That's good news, and I imagine it has benefits beyond the wolves themselves.","So the wolves must have been removed from the park again.","Wolves are more dangerous than bears, aren't they?","That's why farmers near the park have stopped keeping cattle."], a:1, e:"Deniz'in “True, and …” cevabı, kurtların kendileri dışında da faydası olduğunu söyleyen bir yorumu onaylayıp genişletir; ardından manzaranın değiştiği anlatılır. C Deniz'in sözüyle çelişir; D ise bir soru etiketi içerir ama “True, and scientists say …” açıklamasıyla bağlanmaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-05", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-02"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nAylin: How was the seminar on the history of vaccination?\nMert: Fascinating. Apparently, people in China and the Ottoman Empire were protecting themselves against smallpox long before the first vaccine was developed in England.\nAylin: ----\nMert: Exactly. They deliberately exposed healthy people to material from mild cases, which usually produced a less dangerous infection.\nAylin: So the idea was much older than I thought.", o:["So smallpox must never have reached China or the Ottoman Empire.","When will the next seminar take place?","Do you mean they had found their own way of creating immunity?","Was the vaccine invented in England by accident?","I thought smallpox was caused by vaccines."], a:2, e:"“Exactly” cevabı ve ardından anlatılan yöntem, bağışıklık kazanmanın kendi yollarını bulduklarını soran C şıkkını onaylar. A Mert'in sözünden yanlış bir sonuç çıkarır ve “Exactly” ile onaylanamaz; D ise İngiltere'deki aşıya odaklanır, Mert'in açıklamasıyla bağlanmaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-06", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-03"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nResearcher: We found that children who ate breakfast every day scored higher on memory tests.\nSupervisor: Did you take into account differences in family income?\nResearcher: ----\nSupervisor: Good. Otherwise, someone could argue that wealthier families both provide breakfast and support learning in other ways.", o:["Yes, we compared only children from households with similar incomes.","No, we assumed that family income would make no difference.","Most of the children preferred cereal to toast.","Breakfast is often said to be the most important meal of the day.","We are planning to repeat the study with adults."], a:0, e:"“Good. Otherwise …” tepkisi, gelir değişkeninin hesaba katıldığını bildiren olumlu bir cevap gerektirir. B soruya cevap verir ama olumsuzdur; “Good” ile ve “Otherwise” uyarısıyla çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-07", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-04"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nElif: Why do some countries add fluoride to tap water?\nCan: ----\nElif: I see. So it's a cheap way of protecting everyone's teeth, not just the teeth of people who visit a dentist regularly.\nCan: Exactly, although some people object to it on the grounds that it removes personal choice.", o:["Because small amounts of fluoride strengthen tooth enamel and reduce decay across the whole population.","Fluoride is also found naturally in some types of tea.","Many people cannot afford to visit a dentist regularly.","Tap water is usually much cheaper than bottled water.","Some countries have stopped adding it in recent years."], a:0, e:"Elif'in “So it's a cheap way of protecting everyone's teeth” çıkarımı, florürün tüm nüfusta diş çürümesini azalttığını söyleyen A şıkkına dayanır. C konuyla ilgili görünür ama florürün dişleri nasıl koruduğunu açıklamaz ve “Why” sorusuna cevap olmaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-08", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-05"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nBurak: I've been reading about the Vikings. They were apparently much more than raiders.\nZeynep: Really? I've always imagined them as warriors who did little but attack monasteries.\nBurak: ----\nZeynep: That changes the picture considerably. They sound more like merchants and settlers than pirates.\nBurak: Yes, the raids were only one part of the story.", o:["Yes, and they attacked monasteries even more often than we used to think.","Viking ships were usually built from oak.","Most history books about them are written in English.","I'm not sure the Vikings had any contact with other peoples.","Many of them were traders who travelled as far as Baghdad, and others founded farming communities in Iceland."], a:4, e:"Zeynep'in “That changes the picture … more like merchants and settlers” sözü, Vikinglerin tüccar ve yerleşimci olduğunu gösteren yeni bir bilgi gerektirir. A savaşçı imajını güçlendirir, tabloyu değiştirmez; D ise tüccarlık fikriyle çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-09", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-06"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nNil: The government is planning to build a new nuclear power station on the coast.\nEmre: It makes sense. We need electricity that doesn't produce carbon emissions.\nNil: ----\nEmre: Possibly, but wind and solar still can't provide power when the weather is calm or cloudy.", o:["So nuclear power must produce more carbon than coal.","The coast is a popular destination for tourists in summer.","I've never visited a power station myself.","Nuclear power stations are usually built near rivers or the sea.","Wouldn't it be cheaper and faster to invest in wind and solar energy instead?"], a:4, e:"Emre'nin “Possibly, but wind and solar still can't …” cevabı, rüzgâr ve güneş enerjisini alternatif olarak öneren bir itiraza verilmiştir. A Emre'nin karbon salmayan elektrik sözüyle çelişir ve rüzgâr–güneş cevabıyla bağlanmaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-10", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["reading-07","w7-04"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nStudent: Why are researchers so interested in the eyes of octopuses?\nBiologist: Because octopuses seem to perceive colour even though they have only one type of light-sensitive cell, which in principle should make them colour-blind.\nStudent: ----\nBiologist: No one has proved it yet, but one theory is that the unusual shape of their pupils helps them to separate different wavelengths of light.\nStudent: That's remarkable.", o:["Are octopuses more intelligent than dolphins?","How do they manage to do that?","Is it true that octopuses have three hearts?","Why do humans have several types of light-sensitive cell?","Have octopuses always lived in shallow water?"], a:1, e:"Biyoloğun “No one has proved it yet, but one theory is …” cevabı, ahtapotların renkleri nasıl algıladığını soran bir “how” sorusuna verilmiştir. D göz hücreleriyle ilgili olduğu için çekicidir ama cevap insanları değil, ahtapotların gözbebeklerini açıklar."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-11", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["reading-08","w7-02"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nJournalist: You've argued that the fall of the Roman Empire was a slow transformation rather than a sudden collapse.\nHistorian: That's right. In many regions, Roman laws, language and towns survived for centuries after 476.\nJournalist: ----\nHistorian: Not exactly. Trade, living standards and the size of cities did decline sharply in the west; my point is that the change was gradual and uneven.\nJournalist: So it was a decline, but not an overnight one.", o:["When exactly did the Roman Empire begin?","Which Roman emperor do you admire most?","Why did Roman laws disappear so quickly after 476?","Didn't the Romans speak Greek rather than Latin?","Does that mean life simply carried on as before for ordinary people?"], a:4, e:"“Not exactly” ve ardından gelen “did decline sharply” açıklaması, hayatın hiç değişmeden sürüp sürmediğini soran E şıkkını düzeltir. C tarihçinin az önce söylediği “survived for centuries” bilgisiyle çelişir; D evet/hayır sorusudur ama cevabın içeriğiyle bağlanmaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-12", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["reading-09","w7-05"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nManager: Sales of our winter coats have fallen sharply this year, even though our prices haven't changed.\nConsultant: Have you looked at the weather data? Demand for seasonal clothing is very sensitive to temperature.\nManager: ----\nConsultant: There you are, then. People simply don't buy heavy coats when they don't feel cold.\nManager: So it's not our products that are the problem.", o:["Yes, and we've doubled our prices since last year.","Should we start selling summer clothes instead?","Now that you mention it, this has been one of the mildest winters on record.","Our competitors' sales have risen this year.","Most of our customers now shop online."], a:2, e:"“There you are, then” ve “when they don't feel cold” ifadeleri, kışın ılık geçtiğini söyleyen bir açıklamaya verilen tepkidir. A müdürün fiyatların değişmediği sözüyle çelişir; D ise hava açıklamasını zayıflatır, “There you are” ile bağlanmaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-13", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-01","w7-06"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nSelim: Electric scooters are everywhere in the city now. They must be reducing traffic.\nEce: ----\nSelim: True, I hadn't thought of that. If they mostly replace walking, they don't do much for congestion.\nEce: Exactly, and they add to the problem of blocked pavements.", o:["Yes, and they have already replaced most private cars.","I've never ridden one myself.","Not necessarily. Studies show that many riders would otherwise have walked or taken the bus, not driven.","They're quite expensive to rent, aren't they?","The city council built several new roads last year."], a:2, e:"Selim'in “True, I hadn't thought of that. If they mostly replace walking …” cevabı, scooter kullanıcılarının aslında yürüyecek olduğunu söyleyen bir itiraza verilmiştir. A Selim'in görüşünü destekler; ona “I hadn't thought of that” denmez."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-14", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-04","w7-02"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nHakan: I've just read that honey found in ancient Egyptian tombs was still edible after thousands of years.\nSeda: How is that possible?\nHakan: ----\nSeda: So its natural properties act as a kind of preservative.\nHakan: Exactly, and that's why it was used to dress wounds in the ancient world.", o:["The tombs were opened by archaeologists in the nineteenth century.","Ancient Egyptians kept their bees in clay pipes.","Most honey sold today is mixed with sugar syrup.","It was sealed in gold containers, which kept it safe from thieves.","It contains very little water and is quite acidic, so bacteria can't survive in it."], a:4, e:"Seda'nın “So its natural properties act as a kind of preservative” çıkarımı, balın kendi özelliklerini (az su, asitlik) açıklayan E şıkkına dayanır. D balın korunmasına dış bir neden gösterir; “natural properties” çıkarımıyla uyuşmaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-15", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-03"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nEngineer: We've finished designing the new bridge, and it should be able to carry twice the current traffic.\nInspector: Have you tested how it would behave in a strong earthquake?\nEngineer: ----\nInspector: Good. This region has experienced several major earthquakes over the past century, so that is essential.", o:["No, we assumed that earthquakes were unlikely here.","Yes, we ran computer simulations of the strongest earthquakes ever recorded in this region.","The bridge will be painted bright red.","Traffic has increased steadily since the old bridge opened.","We're still deciding what to call it."], a:1, e:"Müfettişin “Good … so that is essential” tepkisi, depreme karşı test yapıldığını bildiren olumlu bir cevap gerektirir. A soruya doğrudan cevap verir ama olumsuzdur ve bölgedeki depremleri hatırlatan “Good” tepkisiyle çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-16", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-06"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nArda: Our city has just banned cars from the historic centre.\nMelis: That should make the air there much cleaner.\nArda: ----\nMelis: Possibly, but many cities that tried it found that shops actually gained customers once the streets became pleasant to walk in.\nArda: I hope that happens here too.", o:["So the historic centre must be more polluted now.","Don't you think shop owners might lose business, though?","I've lived in this city all my life.","The ban came into force last Monday.","Cars have now been banned from the entire country."], a:1, e:"Melis'in “Possibly, but … shops actually gained customers” cevabı, dükkânların müşteri kaybedebileceği yönündeki bir kaygıya verilmiştir. A Melis'in havanın temizleneceği sözüyle çelişir; D bir bilgi verir ama “Possibly, but” ile bağlanmaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-17", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["reading-09"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nStudent: I've been studying for hours every evening, but I seem to forget everything by the next morning.\nAdvisor: How much sleep are you getting? The brain strengthens new memories while we sleep.\nStudent: ----\nAdvisor: That's probably the problem, then. Try to stop studying earlier and get at least seven hours.\nStudent: I'll give it a try.", o:["Not much, to be honest. I usually study until three and get up at seven.","Should I study in the library instead?","About nine hours every night, actually.","Do you think I should give up studying altogether?","Which subjects are the hardest to remember?"], a:0, e:"“That's probably the problem, then” ve “get at least seven hours” önerisi, öğrencinin az uyuduğunu söyleyen bir cevaba tepkidir. C de uyku süresi bildirir ama dokuz saat uyku sorunu açıklamaz ve yedi saat önerisiyle çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-18", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["reading-07"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nVisitor: What is that large machine in the corner of the museum?\nGuide: It's a replica of Charles Babbage's Difference Engine, a mechanical calculator designed in the 1820s but never completed in his lifetime.\nVisitor: ----\nGuide: It can. When engineers finally built one from his original drawings in 1991, it produced results accurate to thirty-one digits.\nVisitor: So his design was sound all along.", o:["Why did Babbage design it in the 1920s?","How much does it cost to visit the museum?","Did Babbage finish building it before he died?","Can a machine built to his plans actually work?","Is it the largest machine in the museum?"], a:3, e:"Rehberin “It can” cevabı “can” ile sorulmuş bir soruyu gerektirir; ardından makinenin doğru çalıştığı anlatılır. C evet/hayır sorusudur ama “Did” ile sorulana “It can” denmez ve rehber zaten tamamlanmadığını söylemiştir."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-19", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["reading-08"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nReporter: Your research found that people who volunteer regularly live longer. Isn't that simply because healthier people are more likely to volunteer?\nResearcher: That was our first concern too, so we compared volunteers with non-volunteers who were equally healthy at the start.\nReporter: ----\nResearcher: Not at all. The benefit remained, although it was smaller than earlier studies had suggested.\nReporter: So volunteering itself seems to make a difference.", o:["How many hours a week do most people volunteer?","Why are healthier people less likely to volunteer?","Do you do any voluntary work yourself?","Which organisations did the volunteers work for?","And did the benefit disappear once you had done that?"], a:4, e:"“Not at all. The benefit remained …” cevabı, sağlık farkı kontrol edilince faydanın kaybolup kaybolmadığını soran E şıkkına verilir. C de evet/hayır sorusudur ama “The benefit remained” açıklamasıyla anlamca bağlanmaz; B ise muhabirin önceki sözünü tersine çevirir."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-20", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["reading-09","w7-03"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nPhysiotherapist: ----\nPatient: Yes, a couple of times. My knee felt fine, so I skipped the exercises for a week, and then the pain came back.\nPhysiotherapist: That often happens. The exercises strengthen the muscles that support the joint, so stopping too early allows the weakness to return.", o:["How long have you had problems with your knee?","Why do you think the pain has come back?","Are you allergic to any painkillers?","Have you ever stopped doing your exercises because your knee felt better?","Would you like to book another appointment next week?"], a:3, e:"Hastanın “Yes, a couple of times” cevabı “Have you ever …?” türünden, sıklık bildiren bir evet/hayır sorusu ister; devamında egzersizleri bıraktığı anlatılır. B konuya uygundur ama “Why” sorusuna “Yes” ile başlayan bir cevap verilmez."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-21", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-04","w7-02"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nOğuz: I watched a documentary about how the Dutch deal with flooding.\nDefne: Don't they just build higher and higher dykes?\nOğuz: ----\nDefne: So instead of only fighting the water, they are giving it space.\nOğuz: Exactly, and some of those areas become parks or wetlands in dry periods.", o:["Not any more. In some places they now lower riverbanks and create areas that are allowed to flood.","Yes, and they plan to build even taller ones everywhere.","The Netherlands is famous for its tulips and windmills.","Much of the country lies below sea level.","Flooding has become rare there because of climate change."], a:0, e:"Defne'nin “So instead of only fighting the water, they are giving it space” çıkarımı, taşmasına izin verilen alanlardan söz eden A şıkkına dayanır. D doğru bir bilgi olsa da suya alan açıldığı sonucunu doğurmaz; B ise bu çıkarımla çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-22", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-05","reading-07"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nCeren: I read that dinosaurs were probably cold-blooded, like modern reptiles.\nTolga: That used to be the common view, but it has been challenged.\nCeren: ----\nTolga: Many of them were covered in feathers, and their bones show growth rates closer to those of birds than of lizards.\nCeren: That changes the picture considerably. They sound far more active than I imagined.", o:["So why did they become extinct?","Are there any reptiles still alive today?","Did birds live at the same time as dinosaurs?","Really? What evidence suggests they were different?","I've always preferred mammals to reptiles."], a:3, e:"Tolga'nın tüyler ve kemik büyüme hızından söz eden cevabı, dinozorların farklı olduğunu gösteren kanıtı soran D şıkkına verilmiştir. C kuşlarla ilgili olduğu için çekicidir ama cevap bir evet/hayır cevabı değil, kanıt listesidir."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-23", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-06","w7-03"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nMehmet: Our university is replacing printed textbooks with digital ones next year.\nAyşe: That will save students a lot of money.\nMehmet: But won't reading on screens make it harder for students to concentrate?\nAyşe: ----\nMehmet: I suppose that's a fair compromise.", o:["No, screens have been proven to improve concentration in every case.","Possibly, but students will still be able to print chapters if they prefer paper.","Printed textbooks are usually cheaper than digital ones.","The library will be closed during the summer.","I've already bought all my books for next year."], a:1, e:"Mehmet'in “that's a fair compromise” sözü, kaygıyı kabul edip ara bir çözüm (isteyene basılı bölüm) sunan B şıkkına uyar. A soruya cevap verir ama uzlaşı değil kesin bir ret içerir ve “in every case” ile abartılıdır; C Ayşe'nin önceki sözüyle çelişir."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-24", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-02"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nLecturer: Most of the energy we get from food ultimately comes from the sun.\nStudent: ----\nLecturer: Exactly. Plants capture sunlight through photosynthesis, and even meat comes from animals that ate plants.\nStudent: So every food chain begins with sunlight.", o:["So eating outdoors in the sun gives us more energy?","Why do some plants grow well in the shade?","How many calories are there in an apple?","Do you mean that even foods that don't come from plants depend on it indirectly?","Is it true that the sun will eventually burn out?"], a:3, e:"“Exactly” onayı ve “even meat comes from animals that ate plants” açıklaması, bitkisel olmayan besinlerin de dolaylı olarak güneşe bağlı olup olmadığını soran D şıkkına uyar. A öğretim görevlisinin sözünü yanlış yorumlar ve “Exactly” ile onaylanamaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-25", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-04"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nİrem: Why do so many astronomers want to build telescopes on the far side of the Moon?\nBatu: ----\nİrem: I see. So the Moon itself would act as a shield.\nBatu: That's right, which would let them detect extremely faint signals from the early universe.", o:["The Moon has no atmosphere, which makes its nights extremely cold.","Building anything on the Moon is extremely expensive.","Because radio interference from Earth never reaches that side, so the sky there is almost completely silent.","Astronauts first landed on the Moon in 1969.","Telescopes on Earth are larger than those in space."], a:2, e:"İrem'in “So the Moon itself would act as a shield” çıkarımı, Dünya'dan gelen radyo parazitinin o tarafa ulaşmadığını söyleyen C şıkkına dayanır. A Ay'ın bir özelliğini verir ama kalkan işlevini açıklamaz; B ise bir gerekçe değil, bir engeldir."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-26", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["w7-03"], q:"Karşılıklı konuşmanın boş bırakılan kısmında söylenmiş olabilecek sözü bulunuz.\n\nChef: I'm planning to add a dish with wild mushrooms to the menu.\nInspector: Where will you get the mushrooms from?\nChef: ----\nInspector: Good. Otherwise you'd risk serving poisonous species that look almost identical to edible ones.", o:["My staff will pick them in the forest near the restaurant.","Only from a licensed supplier whose experts check every batch.","Mushrooms are very popular with our customers.","I haven't decided on the price yet.","They'll be cooked with garlic and butter."], a:1, e:"Müfettişin “Good. Otherwise …” tepkisi, zehirli türler riskini ortadan kaldıran güvenli bir kaynak bildiren B şıkkını gerektirir. A “Where” sorusuna cevap verir ama tam da müfettişin uyardığı riski taşıdığı için “Good” ile uyuşmaz."},
  {id:"r-rd-dialogue-27", sub:"rd-dialogue", from:["reading-08","w7-06"], q:"Diyalogda boş bırakılan yere uygun düşen ifadeyi bulunuz.\n\nInterviewer: In your book, you criticise the way many zoos operate. Are you saying zoos should be closed?\nZoologist: ----\nInterviewer: So you see a future for zoos, but a very different one.\nZoologist: Precisely. Their value lies in conservation, not entertainment.", o:["Not at all. I simply think they should focus on breeding endangered species rather than on entertaining visitors.","Yes, all zoos should be closed immediately.","My book took five years to write.","Most visitors come to see large animals such as elephants.","I visited more than fifty zoos while I was researching it."], a:0, e:"Görüşmecinin “So you see a future for zoos, but a very different one” çıkarımı, hayvanat bahçelerinin kapatılmasını reddedip farklı bir rol öneren A şıkkına dayanır. B soruya doğrudan cevap verir ama “you see a future for zoos” çıkarımıyla çelişir."}
);
